OAI_PAPER({"slug": "An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026", "title": "An <i>L</i><sup>3</sup> bound for the trilinear Hilbert transform", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "086", "family_title": "An <i>L</i><sup>3</sup> bound for the trilinear Hilbert transform", "abstract_html": "We prove that the trilinear Hilbert transform with fixed slopes 1, 2, 3 maps \\(L^3(\\mathbb R)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R)\\) to \\(L^1(\\mathbb R)\\), resolving this case of the trilinear Hilbert transform conjecture.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(f_1,f_2,f_3\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R)\\), define\n  \\[\n  T(f_1,f_2,f_3)(x)\n  =\\operatorname{p.v.}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\n  f_1(x-t)f_2(x-2t)f_3(x-3t)\\,\\frac{dt}{t},\n  \\]\n  where the principal value removes a symmetric interval about the origin.</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout the proof, \\(e(t)=\\exp(2\\pi it)\\), progression coordinates are \\(y_j=x+jt\\), \\(0\\le j\\le3\\), and inner products are linear in the first argument.</p><p class=\"li\">Interval-subscripted norms and inner products use normalized Lebesgue measure. Norms without an interval subscript use ordinary Lebesgue measure.</p><p class=\"li\">All auxiliary dyadic trees are finite. Depth increases by one when interval length is halved. All estimates are required to be uniform in the number of depths.</p><p class=\"li\">For an interval \\(I\\) of length \\(r\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_I(z_0,z_1,z_2,z_3)\n  =r^{-2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}w_I(x,t)\\prod_{j=0}^3z_j(x+jt)\\,dx\\,dt.\n  \\]\n  Fix \\(c_0&gt;0\\) and \\(C_0\\ge1\\). A kernel is admissible if:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. every \\(x+jt\\) on its support lies in \\(I\\), at distance at least \\(c_0r\\) from the endpoints;</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. for every \\(u\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(0\\le j\\le3\\),\n     \\[\n     \\int_{\\mathbb R}w_I(u-jt,t)\\,dt=0;\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. every derivative of total order \\(n\\) is bounded by\n     \\[\n     r^{-n}(C_0(n+2))^{C_0(n+2)}.\n     \\]\n  Kernels at different intervals may be chosen independently and multiplied by unimodular scalars. Vector-valued forms use only the first, or physical, scalar coordinate.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(M_qf=(M(|f|^q))^{1/q}\\), where \\(M\\) is the uncentered Hardy–Littlewood maximal operator. For \\(2&lt;q&lt;3\\), let \\(C_N(q)\\) be the least constant such that\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal I}|I|\\,|H_I(f_0,f_1,f_2,f_3)|\n  \\le C_N(q)\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\prod_{j=0}^3M_qf_j\n  \\]\n  for finite collections in at most \\(N\\) consecutive depths, every dyadic lattice, every admissible kernel family, and compactly supported \\(L^q\\) inputs.</p><p class=\"li\">A rational number has height at most \\(B\\) if it has a representation \\(p/q\\), \\(q&gt;0\\), with \\(\\max(|p|,q)\\le B\\). Vector and matrix heights are entrywise.</p><p class=\"li\">“Quasipolynomial” means a bound\n  \\[\n  \\exp\\!\\left(C_q\\left(1+\\sum_a\\log H_a+\\sum_b\\log(1/\\delta_b)\\right)^C\\right);\n  \\]\n  “polylogarithmic” means the expression without the outer exponential. The degrees denoted by \\(C\\) are independent of \\(q\\), spatial lengths, frequencies, and the number of scales.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite constant \\(C\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|T(f_1,f_2,f_3)\\|_{L^1(\\mathbb R)}\n\\le C\\prod_{j=1}^3\\|f_j\\|_{L^3(\\mathbb R)}.\n\\]\nConsequently, \\(T\\) has a unique bounded trilinear extension\n\\[\nL^3(\\mathbb R)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R)\n\\longrightarrow L^1(\\mathbb R).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 14 establishes normalized absorption for a suitable \\(q\\in(2,3)\\). Proposition 15 removes the normalization, absorbs the small coefficient of the finite constants \\(C_N(q)\\), and obtains the uniform local estimate. Its continuous-kernel reduction and duality then prove\n\\[\n\\|T(f_1,f_2,f_3)\\|_1\\le C\\prod_{j=1}^3\\|f_j\\|_3.\n\\]\nThe density and completeness argument in Proposition 15 gives the asserted unique bounded extension.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 3.4, Compression", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J\\) be the root of a finite dyadic tree and \\(\\mathcal V\\) a finite label set, with no bound on its total cardinality.</p><p class=\"li\">Label \\(v\\) has birth cell \\(J_v\\subset J\\), scalar function \\(\\phi_v\\), private weight \\(\\epsilon_v&gt;0\\), and augmented column\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_v(y)=(\\phi_v(y),\\sqrt{\\epsilon_v}e_v)\n  \\quad(y\\in J_v)\n  \\]\n  in \\(\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C\\oplus\\ell^2(\\mathcal V)\\). Let \\(\\widetilde\\Phi_v=1_{J_v}\\Phi_v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A common budget \\(D\\ge2\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\|\\Phi_v\\|_{L^\\infty(J_v;\\mathcal H)}\\le D,\\qquad\n  \\sqrt{\\epsilon_v}\\ge D^{-1},\\qquad\n  \\sum_v1_{J_v}(y)\\le D\\quad\\text{a.e.}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(1\\le i\\le m\\), masks \\(\\mathcal V^i(I)\\subset\\{v:I\\subset J_v\\}\\) are inherited:\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal V^i(I)\\subset\\mathcal V^i(I')\n  \\quad\\text{when }I'\\text{ is a child of }I.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(P_I^i\\) be orthogonal projection onto constant-coefficient combinations of the available columns on \\(I\\), extended by zero outside \\(I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Rows \\(B_\\nu:L^2(J;\\mathcal H)\\to\\mathcal Y_\\nu\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\sum_\\nu\\|B_\\nu z\\|_{\\mathcal Y_\\nu}^2\\le\\|z\\|_2^2,\n  \\qquad B_\\nu=B_\\nu1_{u(\\nu)},\n  \\]\n  where \\(u(\\nu)\\) is a tree cell.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>An absolute \\(C\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_\\nu\n\\|B_\\nu P_{u(\\nu)}^m\\cdots P_{u(\\nu)}^1z\\|^2\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le\n\\bigl(C(m+1)^C\\log(2D)\\bigr)^{C(m+1)}\\|z\\|_2.\n\\]\nThe estimate is uniform in row cells, total labels, and spatial depth. Identity factors may be omitted. Products containing \\(1-P^i\\) obey the same form of bound. Integrating compositions against a fixed finite measure costs at most its total variation in addition.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a nonempty product, add a final generation below all row cells and write\n\\[\na_K=\\fint_K\\|z\\|.\n\\]\nStop whenever this average first exceeds twice the current root average. Let \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the resulting roots and \\(\\mathcal T_K\\) the intervening size segment. Cut each segment at its next roots and final leaves.</p><p>On each cut cell, project \\(z\\) onto all first-layer columns used along its segment path. The resulting representative \\(g_K\\), extended by zero outside \\(K\\), satisfies, with \\(G=4D^5\\),\n\\[\n\\|g_K\\|_\\infty\\le Ga_K,\\qquad\n\\|g_K\\|_2^2=\\langle z,g_K\\rangle,\\qquad\nP_u^1g_K=P_u^1z\\quad(u\\in\\mathcal T_K).\n\\]\nThe first two conclusions follow from Lemma 1.2; the last follows by adding the column moment identities over the cut partition of \\(u\\).</p><p>Doubling stopping averages and the dyadic maximal inequality give\n\\[\n\\sum_{K\\in\\mathcal S}a_K^2|K|\\le \\frac{16}{3}\\|z\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\nS:=\\sum_K\\|g_K\\|_2^2\\le C\\log(2D)\\|z\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nIndeed, at a point \\(y\\), roots with\n\\(a_K&lt;M_d\\|z\\|(y)/(GD^2)\\) contribute at most\n\\(2D^{-2}M_d\\|z\\|(y)\\) to \\(\\sum_K\\|g_K(y)\\|\\).\nThe other roots number at most\n\\(L_D=2+\\lceil\\log_2(GD^2)\\rceil\\).\nThe moment identity and Cauchy–Schwarz yield\n\\[\nS\\le4D^{-2}\\|z\\|_2^2+\\sqrt{L_D}\\|z\\|_2\\sqrt S,\n\\]\nwhich proves the claim.</p><p>Fix a segment \\(K\\), write \\(g=g_K\\), \\(a=a_K\\), and choose\n\\[\n\\tau=D^{-10},\\quad b=2D(m+1),\\quad\nT=\\left\\lceil b^{128(m+1)^2}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nLet \\(c(u)\\) be the last-layer coefficient vector of\n\\(P_u^m\\cdots P_u^1g\\).\nStarting at an attempt root \\(R\\), declare anchors at the first descendants for which the coefficient vector moves by more than \\(\\tau a\\). At the \\(T\\)-th transition send the cell and descendants to a new attempt.</p><p>Complete anchor generations to refining stopping partitions. Their direct-sum projection families are nested, so Lemma 1.3 applies. On a new root \\(R'\\), every transition has pointwise norm greater than \\(D^{-1}\\tau a\\), by the private coordinates. Thus\n\\[\nTD^{-2}\\tau^2a^2\\sum_{R'}|R'|\n\\le m^2L_T^{2m}\\|1_Rg\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\|g\\|_\\infty\\le Ga\\), both length and energy contract:\n\\[\n\\sum_{R'}|R'|\\le E|R|,\\qquad\n\\sum_{R'}\\|1_{R'}g\\|_2^2\\le E\\|1_Rg\\|_2^2,\n\\quad\nE=\\frac{16D^{32}m^2L_T^{2m}}T&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe paper verifies this from \\(L_T\\le129(m+1)^2\\log_2b\\le b^4\\).\nSumming attempts gives total root length at most \\(2|K|\\) and representative energy at most \\(2\\|g_K\\|_2^2\\).</p><p>Assign each row to its last attempt and last anchor. Lemma 1.3 bounds anchor contributions by \\(L_T^{2m}\\|1_Rg_K\\|_2^2\\). The coefficient error has \\(\\ell^2\\) norm at most \\(\\tau a_K\\). Locality gives\n\\[\nB_\\nu\\!\\left(1_{u(\\nu)}\\sum_ve_{\\nu,v}\\Phi_v\\right)\n=\\sum_ve_{\\nu,v}B_\\nu(1_R\\widetilde\\Phi_v).\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz in labels and row contraction imply squared error at most\n\\[\n\\tau^2a_K^2\\sum_v\\|1_R\\widetilde\\Phi_v\\|_2^2\n\\le\\tau^2a_K^2D^3|R|.\n\\]\nHere the last inequality integrates the pathwise label budget and does not bound labels on disjoint branches.</p><p>Summing segments and attempts,\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_\\nu\\|B_\\nu P_{u(\\nu)}^m\\cdots P_{u(\\nu)}^1z\\|^2\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le C\\bigl(L_T^m\\sqrt{\\log(2D)}+D^{-17/2}\\bigr)\\|z\\|_2.\n\\]\nSince \\(L_T\\le C(m+1)^3\\log(2D)\\), this is the stated estimate. Expand complements into at most \\(2^m\\) ordered products. Restore fixed averages by the integral triangle inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, Packing of attempts", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>A construction starting at a root \\(J\\) sends exceptional cells and all their descendants to estimates on disjoint subroots. At each root \\(R\\), the new roots \\(R'\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_{R'\\text{ child root of }R}|R'|\\le\\rho|R|,\n\\qquad0\\le\\rho&lt;1.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The sum of all root lengths, including \\(J\\), is at most \\(|J|/(1-\\rho)\\). If an attempt from \\(R\\) contributes at most \\(A|R|\\), and its hypotheses hold at each new root, the total contribution is at most \\(A|J|/(1-\\rho)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The sum of the root lengths in generation \\(n\\) is at most \\(\\rho^n|J|\\), by induction. Summing this geometric series proves both assertions.</p><p>Several stopping requirements may be combined by taking maximal intervals in the union of their exceptional-root families. Their total relative cost is at most the sum of the individual costs.</p><p>A stopping boundary for summation need not be a boundary for an auxiliary projection family: continue an inherited column list on all descendants of a terminal decision cell by retaining its columns and refitting constant coefficients. The resulting refining-partition projections remain nested and are \\(L^2(I)\\) contractions even when finer input averages are not normalized.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, Coefficient and projection bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the column system and budget of Proposition 1. Let \\(A\\) be any set of columns available on a cell \\(I\\), and let \\(P_{I,A}\\) project onto their span.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a.e. \\(y\\in I\\),\n\\[\nD^{-1}\\Bigl(\\sum_{v\\in A}|c_v|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2}\n\\le\\Bigl\\|\\sum_{v\\in A}c_v\\Phi_v(y)\\Bigr\\|_{\\mathcal H}\n\\le D^{3/2}\\Bigl(\\sum_{v\\in A}|c_v|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nIn particular, coefficients are unique, and\n\\[\n\\|P_{I,A}z\\|_\\infty\\le D^5\\fint_I\\|z(y)\\|_{\\mathcal H}\\,dy.\n\\]\nFor \\(F=(f,0)\\) and \\(P_{I,A}F=\\sum_vc_v\\Phi_v\\),\n\\[\n\\fint_I\\bigl(f-(P_{I,A}F)_{\\rm phys}\\bigr)\\overline{\\phi_v}\n=\\epsilon_vc_v.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The private component has squared norm \\(\\sum_v\\epsilon_v|c_v|^2\\), giving the lower bound. The upper bound follows from Cauchy–Schwarz, since at most \\(D\\) columns occur and each has norm at most \\(D\\).</p><p>Let \\(E_yc=\\sum_vc_v\\Phi_v(y)\\) and \\(G=\\fint_IE_y^*E_y\\,dy\\). Then\n\\[\nG\\ge D^{-2}{\\rm Id},\\qquad\\|E_y\\|\\le D^{3/2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nP_{I,A}z(y)=\\fint_IE_yG^{-1}E_t^*z(t)\\,dt,\n\\qquad\\|E_yG^{-1}E_t^*\\|\\le D^5.\n\\]\nThis proves the supremum estimate. Orthogonality to \\(\\Phi_v\\) gives the last identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, Products of nested prefixes", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\mathcal X\\) be a Hilbert space. For \\(1\\le i\\le m\\), let \\(P_0^i,\\ldots,P_T^i\\) be nested orthogonal projections. Put\n\\[\nZ_n=P_n^m\\cdots P_n^1,\\qquad\nL_T=1+\\lceil\\log_2(T+1)\\rceil.\n\\]\nLet finitely many rows satisfy \\(\\sum_\\nu\\|B_\\nu h\\|^2\\le\\|h\\|^2\\), and choose \\(n(\\nu)\\in\\{0,\\ldots,T\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left(\\sum_\\nu\\|B_\\nu Z_{n(\\nu)}g\\|^2\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le L_T^m\\|g\\|,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^T\\|(Z_n-Z_{n-1})g\\|^2\n\\le m^2L_T^{2(m-1)}\\|g\\|^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(m=0\\), the first estimate has constant one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\Delta_0^i=P_0^i\\) and \\(\\Delta_n^i=P_n^i-P_{n-1}^i\\). Within each family these are pairwise orthogonal projections. Partition every prefix into dyadic index blocks with at most one block of each length, and write \\(\\Delta_A^i=\\sum_{n\\in A}\\Delta_n^i\\).</p><p>Expand every factor of \\(Z_{n(\\nu)}\\). For a fixed tuple of block lengths, group rows having the same tuple of blocks. The row contraction bounds their squared sum by\n\\(\\|\\Delta_{A_m}^m\\cdots\\Delta_{A_1}^1g\\|^2\\). Summing block tuples and using successive Bessel inequalities gives at most \\(\\|g\\|^2\\). There are at most \\(L_T^m\\) length tuples; the triangle inequality in the row Hilbert sum proves the first estimate. No commutation between layers is used.</p><p>For the second estimate,\n\\[\nZ_n-Z_{n-1}\n=\\sum_{i=1}^mP_n^m\\cdots P_n^{i+1}\\Delta_n^i\nP_{n-1}^{i-1}\\cdots P_{n-1}^1.\n\\]\nDiscard the left contractions, apply Cauchy–Schwarz in \\(i\\), and use the first estimate with rows \\(B_n=\\Delta_n^i\\). This gives\n\\[\n\\sum_n\\|(Z_n-Z_{n-1})g\\|^2\n\\le m\\sum_{i=1}^mL_T^{2(i-1)}\\|g\\|^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, Height bins", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(d\\ge1\\), \\(B\\ge2\\), \\(\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^d\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathcal E=\\{|a\\cdot\\xi|:a\\in\\mathbb Q^d\n\\text{ has height at most }B,\\ a\\cdot\\xi\\ne0\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The set \\(\\{\\log_2v:v\\in\\mathcal E\\}\\) is covered by at most \\(d\\) intervals, each of length at most \\(C(d+1)^3\\log(2B)\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal E\\) meets at most \\(C(d+1)^4\\log(2B)\\) dyadic magnitude bins.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(r_n=r_02^{-n}\\), \\(r_0&gt;0\\), \\(\\ell\\in\\{1,2\\}\\), and \\(0&lt;\\alpha\\le\\beta\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\#\\{n:\\exists v\\in\\mathcal E,\\ \\alpha\\le r_n^\\ell v\\le\\beta\\}\n   \\le C(d+1)^4\\bigl(\\log(2B)+\\log(2\\beta/\\alpha)\\bigr).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\n   \\max\\bigl(\\{r_n^\\ell v:v\\in\\mathcal E,\\ r_n^\\ell v\\le\\varepsilon\\}\\cup\\{0\\}\\bigr)\n   \\le C(d+1)^4\\log(2B)\\,\\varepsilon.\n   \\]\nThe conclusions also apply to suprema of finitely many coordinate evaluations. For Euclidean norms of vectors with at most \\(h\\) coordinates, add \\(Cd\\log(2h)\\) to the bin and scale counts and to the coefficient of \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In a minimal rational dependence among height-\\(B\\) vectors \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_k\\), \\(k\\le d+1\\), clear denominators in each vector and take signed maximal minors on independent coordinate rows. This gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^kc_ia_i=0,\\qquad\nc_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\},\\qquad\n|c_i|\\le H:=d!B^{d^2+d}.\n\\]\nPut \\(R=4dH\\). Vectors whose nonzero evaluation magnitudes satisfy\n\\(s_{i+1}&gt;Rs_i\\) must be independent: in a minimal dependence the largest term has magnitude at least \\(s_*\\), whereas all other terms together have magnitude less than \\(dHs_*/R&lt;s_*\\).</p><p>Greedily cover \\(\\mathcal E\\) by intervals \\([s,Rs]\\), always choosing the smallest uncovered value. At most \\(d\\) intervals are needed, and\n\\[\n\\log_2R\\le C(d+1)^3\\log(2B).\n\\]\nThis proves the first assertion. Each such logarithmic packet permits only an interval of scale indices of length\n\\((\\log_2R+\\log_2(\\beta/\\alpha))/\\ell\\), proving the second.</p><p>For a bin \\([2^j,2^{j+1})\\), let \\(n_0\\) be the first index with \\(r_{n_0}^\\ell2^j\\le\\varepsilon\\). Its total tail contribution is at most\n\\[\n2\\varepsilon\\sum_{a\\ge0}2^{-\\ell a}\\le4\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nSum over occupied bins. Suprema are coordinate magnitudes; a Euclidean norm lies between the coordinate supremum and \\(\\sqrt h\\) times that supremum. Enlarging each packet by \\(\\frac12\\log_2h\\) proves the last assertion. The estimates allow a new bounded-height rational coefficient vector at every depth and require no bound on \\(\\xi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 4.12, Local detection", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the local forms and normalized interval norms in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A quadratic chart atom, as in original Definition 4.1, is\n  \\[\n  \\phi(y)=e(\\lambda y^2+\\beta y)\n  \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z^d}\\chi(\\theta y-n)\n  e\\bigl((\\theta y)^TC(\\theta y-n)\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  Here \\(\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^d\\), \\(\\lambda,\\beta\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(C\\) is rational, and \\(\\chi\\) is smooth and compactly supported. Dimension zero includes ordinary quadratic phases.</p><p class=\"li\">Complexity at most \\(H\\ge e^2\\) means\n  \\[\n  d\\le\\log H,\\quad \\operatorname{height}(C)\\le H,\\quad\n  \\operatorname{supp}\\chi\\subset[-H,H]^d,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\|\\partial^\\alpha\\chi\\|_\\infty\n  \\le H^{c_0(|\\alpha|+1)}(|\\alpha|+1)^{c_0(|\\alpha|+1)}\n  \\]\n  for one fixed Gevrey constant \\(c_0\\). Real oscillation parameters are unrestricted. A bounded atom has supremum norm at most one.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(q&gt;2\\), \\(C_1\\ge1\\), and \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Functions \\(R,h,u,v\\), in any four distinct slots, satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\|R\\|_{2,I}\\le C_1,\\quad \\|h\\|_{q,I}\\le1,\\quad\n  \\|u\\|_{2,I},\\|v\\|_{2,I}\\le1,\\quad\n  |H_I(R,h,u,v)|&gt;\\delta.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a bounded atom \\(\\phi\\) of complexity at most\n\\[\nD\\le\\exp\\!\\left(C_q(1+\\log C_1+\\log(1/\\delta))^C\\right)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\left|\\fint_IR(y)\\overline{\\phi(y)}\\,dy\\right|\\ge D^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe exponent \\(C\\) is independent of \\(q\\). The atom may be conjugated for a bilinear moment convention.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(q'=q/(q-1)&lt;2\\). Clip each opposing input at height \\(B\\). The tails satisfy\n\\[\n\\|h1_{|h|&gt;B}\\|_2\\le B^{-(q-2)/2},\\qquad\n\\|u1_{|u|&gt;B}\\|_{q'},\\|v1_{|v|&gt;B}\\|_{q'}\n\\le B^{-(q-2)/q}.\n\\]\nLemma 3.1 with exponents \\((2,2,2,2)\\) and \\((2,q,q',2)\\) bounds total clipping error by \\(CC_1B^{-(q-2)/q}\\). Choose\n\\[\nB=\\max\\bigl(2,(100CC_1/\\delta)^{q/(q-2)}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe clipped form exceeds \\(3\\delta/4\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 3.10 to \\(h/B\\) with\n\\(\\tau_1=\\delta/(100CC_1B^3)\\).\nAfter multiplying back, the structured part \\(s_1\\) has coefficient sum\n\\(A_1=BD_{\\tau_1}\\).\nLemma 3.11 bounds each uniform and \\(L^1\\) error by \\(CC_1B^3\\tau_1\\).\nApply the decomposition next to \\(u/B\\) with\n\\(\\tau_2=\\delta/(100CC_1A_1B^2)\\), obtaining coefficient sum\n\\(A_2=BD_{\\tau_2}\\).\nSum its errors over the atoms of \\(s_1\\). Then\n\\[\n|H_I(R,s_1,s_2,v_B)|&gt;\\delta/4.\n\\]\nSome atom pair therefore gives a form exceeding\n\\(\\delta_0=\\delta/(4A_1A_2)\\).</p><p>Use Lemma 3.5, discard shells above \\(T_0\\) with\n\\(CC_1D_0T_0^{-100}&lt;\\delta_0/4\\), and select a retained term exceeding\n\\(\\delta_1=\\delta_0/(2D_0)\\).\nLemma 3.6 forces \\(A_r\\ge\\delta_1/(CC_1)\\) and gives a separated kernel approximation with error at most \\(\\delta_1/(10CC_1)\\). Hence the surviving form is a finite sum of products of moments:\n\\[\n\\sum_\\nu c_\\nu\n\\left(\\fint_IR\\varphi_{\\nu,a}\\right)\n\\left(\\fint_Iv_B\\varphi_{\\nu,b}\\right).\n\\]\nThe second moments are at most one. The coefficient sum and atom complexities are quasipolynomial, so one first moment is at least \\(\\delta_1/(2D_1)\\).</p><p>There are only two bounded-input decompositions and a fixed number of chart operations. The \\(q\\)-dependent clipping power multiplies an accuracy logarithm by a \\(q\\)-dependent constant, leaving an absolute logarithmic exponent. This proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, Local absolute estimate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(p_j\\ge1\\) and \\(\\sum_{j=0}^3p_j^{-1}\\le2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nr^{-2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}|w_I(x,t)|\n\\prod_{j=0}^3\\|z_j(x+jt)\\|\\,dx\\,dt\n\\lesssim\\prod_{j=0}^3\\|z_j\\|_{p_j,I}.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(C_N(q)\\lesssim N\\), so every \\(C_N(q)\\) used below is finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>For distinct \\(i,j\\), the change of variables \\((x,t)\\mapsto(y_i,y_j)\\) has nonzero constant determinant. This proves the endpoints with two exponents equal to one and the others infinity. The all-infinity estimate follows from support and kernel bounds; one-exponent endpoints follow by normalized norm monotonicity. Log-convexity fills the polytope\n\\(0\\le p_j^{-1}\\le1\\), \\(\\sum_jp_j^{-1}\\le2\\).</p><p>For \\(C_N\\), take all exponents two. Local \\(L^2\\) norms are bounded by local \\(L^q\\) norms, whose product is at most \\(\\prod_jM_qf_j\\) at every point of \\(I\\). Sum disjoint intervals at each depth.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, Elementary chart operations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Statement.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Complex conjugation, a product of a bounded number of atoms, and translation or affine rescaling of the real argument preserve the chart class, with a quasipolynomial complexity change. The same holds for multivariable charts with \\(\\theta y\\) replaced by \\(Vz\\). Only unrestricted real oscillation parameters may depend on the translation or rescaling size.</p>", "proof": "<p>Conjugation negates \\(C,\\lambda,\\beta\\). Products use direct sums of horizontal coordinates and block diagonal matrices, with derivative bounds from the product rule.</p><p>For a translated horizontal argument \\(\\theta y+a\\), choose \\(P\\) clearing \\(C\\), with \\(P\\le H^{d^2}\\), and write \\(a=m_0+a_0\\), \\(m_0\\in P\\mathbb Z^d\\), \\(\\|a_0\\|_\\infty\\le P\\). Reindex by \\(n+m_0\\). For \\(w=\\theta y-n\\), modulo integers the new phase is\n\\[\n(\\theta y)^TCw+(\\theta y)^TCa_0+m_0^TC\\theta y\n+a_0^TC(w+a_0)+m_0^TCa_0.\n\\]\nThe linear terms alter the unrestricted linear phase, the last term is constant, and the bounded \\(a_0\\)-dependent term is absorbed into the smooth amplitude. Ordinary quadratic translation and rescaling have the asserted effects. The same calculation works for \\(Vz\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, Scale-wise Weyl alternative", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For \\(1\\le k\\le3\\), let\n\\[\nQ(z)=e(z^T\\Lambda z+b^Tz)\n\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z^d}\\chi(Vz-n)e((Vz)^TC(Vz-n)),\n\\]\nwith chart complexity at most \\(H\\), \\(\\Lambda\\) real symmetric, and \\(V\\) real. Put\n\\[\nA=C-C^T,\\quad C_{\\rm sy}=(C+C^T)/2,\\quad\nC_{\\rm sk}=(C-C^T)/2.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\vartheta\\) be supported in a fixed box, with supremum and Lipschitz norms at most \\(H\\), and suppose\n\\[\n\\sup_\\xi\\left|\\int\\vartheta(z)Q(z)e(-\\xi^Tz)\\,dz\\right|\\ge\\delta,\n\\quad0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a rational subspace \\(U\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\), with orthogonal projections \\(P_f\\) onto \\(U\\), \\(P_s=1-P_f\\), such that\n\\[\nA|_{U\\times U}=0,\\qquad \\|V_s\\|,\\|\\Lambda_*\\|\\le H',\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nV_f=P_fV,\\quad V_s=P_sV,\\quad\n\\Lambda_*=\\Lambda+\\tfrac12V^TC_{\\rm sy}V\n+\\operatorname{sym}(V_f^TC_{\\rm sk}V_s),\n\\]\nand the heights of \\(P_f,P_s\\) and the bound \\(H'\\) are at most\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\left(C(1+\\log H+\\log(1/\\delta))^C\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(P\\) clear \\(C\\), put \\(F=\\vartheta Q\\), and bound its support, supremum, \\(L^2\\) norm, and first absolute moment by a quasipolynomial \\(D\\). For a return\n\\[\nVh=m+d_h,\\qquad m\\in P\\mathbb Z^d,\n\\]\ntranslation and lattice reindexing give\n\\[\nF(z+h)=e(c(h,m))e(\\Omega(h,m)^Tz)F(z)+E_{h,m}(z),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\Omega(h,m)=(2\\Lambda+V^TCV)h-V^TAm,\n\\quad\n\\|E_{h,m}\\|_1+\\|E_{h,m}\\|_\\infty\n\\le D(\\|h\\|+\\|d_h\\|).\n\\]\nFor \\(u=Vz\\), \\(w=u-n\\), the nonconstant chart increment is\n\\(u^TCd_h+m^TCu+d_h^TCw\\); the discarded \\(m^TCn\\) is integral.</p><p>Choose a Fourier value at least \\(3\\delta/4\\), radius \\(\\rho=\\delta/(100D)\\), an integer\n\\(M\\ge100D^2\\delta^{-2}\\rho^{-k}\\), and a quasipolynomially small \\(a&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\na\\le\\delta/(100DM),\\qquad\na\\le1/(100(1+\\|A\\|)).\n\\]\nFor a return below \\(a\\), multiples \\(1,\\ldots,M\\) give Fourier values at least \\(\\delta/2\\) at increments \\(\\Omega\\). If \\(\\|\\Omega\\|\\ge1\\), disjoint radius-\\(\\rho\\) balls contradict Plancherel. Hence \\(\\|\\Omega\\|&lt;1\\). Antisymmetrization gives\n\\[\nh'^T\\Omega(h,m)-h^T\\Omega(h',m')\n=d_{h'}^TAd_h-m'^TAm.\n\\]\nThe final term is integral and has magnitude less than one, so it vanishes.</p><p>For \\(d\\ge1\\), use the symmetric convex body\n\\[\n\\mathcal K=P^{-1}V[-1,1]^k+[-1,1]^d.\n\\]\nIts successive minima and those of its polar satisfy\n\\(1\\le\\lambda_j\\mu_{d+1-j}\\le(d!)^2\\). The upper bound follows from Minkowski’s second theorem and\n\\(\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal K)\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal K^\\circ)\\ge4^d/(d!)^2\\), proved using a maximal-determinant coordinate parallelotope and its inscribed cross-polytopes.</p><p>Put \\(L=16(d!)^2\\). Among \\(d+1\\) successive intervals\n\\((b_{\\max}L^{-j-1},b_{\\max}L^{-j}]\\), choose one with no minimum, with left endpoint \\(b_0\\), and set\n\\[\nU=\\operatorname{span}(\\mathbb Z^d\\cap b_0\\mathcal K).\n\\]\nThese lattice points give small returns, so \\(U\\) is isotropic. Transference supplies bounded integer vectors spanning \\(U^\\perp\\). If their rows form \\(S\\),\n\\[\nP_s=S^T(SS^T)^{-1}S.\n\\]\nCramer’s rule bounds its height and \\(P_sV\\). If \\(U=\\mathbb R^d\\), take \\(P_s=0\\).</p><p>Finally, torus pigeonholing on each coordinate line gives a return\n\\(h=t_je_j\\), with \\(t_j^{-1}\\) quasipolynomially bounded and \\(m\\in U\\). Isotropy controls\n\\(V^TA(m-V_fh)=V_s^TA(m-V_fh)\\). The identity\n\\[\n2\\Lambda+V^TCV-V^TAV_f\n=2\\Lambda_*+V_s^TC_{\\rm sk}V_s\n\\]\nthen bounds each column of \\(\\Lambda_*\\). For \\(d=0\\), the translation argument directly bounds \\(2\\Lambda h\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.4, Chart expansion and uniform tails", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Lemma 3.3 and suppose a rational orthogonal splitting has heights and indicated sizes at most \\(B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a fixed box,\n\\[\nQ(z)=e(z^T\\Lambda_*z+b^Tz)\\sum_\\ell a_\\ell e(\\sigma_\\ell^TVz)\n\\]\nis an exact absolutely convergent expansion, consisting of quasipolynomially many Fourier-series families in at most \\(2d\\) integer indices. The coefficient sum is at most \\(D\\), quasipolynomial in \\(H,B\\), and rational frequencies have height at most \\(D(1+\\|\\ell\\|)\\).</p><p>For fixed integer \\(J\\ge1\\), dyadic shells satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_{T\\le1+\\|\\ell\\|&lt;2T}|a_\\ell|\n\\le D_JT^{-J(d+1)^2},\n\\]\nwith \\(D_J\\) quasipolynomial. For fixed underlying oscillation parameters, the same holds for coefficient envelopes pooled over rational projection choices of height at most \\(B\\), separately by frequency and effective quadratic part, uniformly over allowed amplitudes and bounded slow values. A quasipolynomial truncation makes the pooled tail at most any prescribed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(u=Vz\\), \\(u_f=P_fu\\), \\(u_s=P_su\\), \\(w=u-n\\). Then\n\\[\nu^TC_{\\rm sy}(u-n)\n=\\tfrac12u^TC_{\\rm sy}u+\\tfrac12w^TC_{\\rm sy}w\n-\\tfrac12n^TC_{\\rm sy}n,\n\\]\nand isotropy gives\n\\[\nu^TC_{\\rm sk}w\n=u_f^TC_{\\rm sk}u_s-u_f^TC_{\\rm sk}P_sn+u_s^TC_{\\rm sk}w.\n\\]\nThe first quadratic terms produce \\(\\Lambda_*\\).</p><p>Choose an even denominator-clearing period \\(P_0\\) for \\(C_{\\rm sy}\\) and a denominator \\(q_0\\) for \\(P_s\\). Split the lattice sum according to\n\\(P_sn=N\\) and \\(n\\equiv a\\pmod{P_0\\mathbb Z^d}\\). There are quasipolynomially many possibilities. Remove the residue phase and \\(-u_f^TC_{\\rm sk}N\\). With a slow cutoff \\(\\eta\\) and an equality cutoff \\(E\\), supported at radius \\(1/(3q_0)\\), the remaining expression is represented by the smooth periodic function\n\\[\n\\eta(s)\\sum_{n\\in a+P_0\\mathbb Z^d}\n\\chi(u-n)E(s-P_s(u-n)-N)\ne\\!\\left(\\tfrac12(u-n)^TC_{\\rm sy}(u-n)+s^TC_{\\rm sk}(u-n)\\right).\n\\]\nAt \\(s=P_su\\), the equality cutoff is the exact indicator of \\(P_sn=N\\).</p><p>Periodize in \\(s\\) with controlled period \\(P_1\\). Fourier modes\n\\(e(p^Ts/P_1+\\ell^Tu/P_0)\\) yield\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\sigma_N+P_s^Tp/P_1+\\ell/P_0.\n\\]\nDerivatives of order \\(m\\), after unit-period rescaling, are bounded by\n\\(D_0^{C(m+d+1)}(m+1)^{C(m+1)}\\).\nIntegrating by parts in a largest Fourier coordinate and taking\n\\(m\\ge J(d+1)^2+4d+4\\) proves the shell estimate and absolute convergence.</p><p>There are at most \\(B^{C(d+1)^2}\\) rational projection matrices. Sum uniform coefficient envelopes over this finite list and over the controlled \\((N,a)\\). Increasing the derivative order absorbs bounded-height frequency counts. Summing shells beyond a quasipolynomial cutoff proves the pooled-tail assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "Lemma 4.5, Progression separation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let bounded atoms \\(\\phi_i,\\phi_k\\), of complexity at most \\(H\\), occupy distinct slots \\(i,k\\). Let \\(a,b\\) be the complementary slots.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Their product has an exact absolutely convergent expansion into terms\n\\[\nc\\,\\psi_a(y_a)\\psi_b(y_b)Q(t).\n\\]\nAll three factors are bounded atoms independent of interval scale. For quasipolynomial \\(D\\), terms may be arranged in dyadic shells \\(T\\ge1\\) with coefficient mass at most \\(DT^{-100}\\), factor dimension at most \\(\\log D\\), and complexity at most \\((DT)^C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First separate \\(\\phi_i\\). Suppress its linear phase and choose fixed rationals\n\\(\\alpha_i=1,\\alpha_k=0,\\alpha_a,\\alpha_b\\) with\n\\(\\sum_j\\alpha_j=\\sum_jj\\alpha_j=0\\).\nInsert nonnegative Gevrey partitions at \\(\\theta y_a,\\theta y_b,\\theta t\\). Their lifts \\(d_a,d_b,d\\), together with the original lift \\(d_i\\), satisfy\n\\[\nk_j=d_j-d_a-(j-a)d\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\quad(j=i,b).\n\\]\nOnly quasipolynomially many carries occur. Smooth equality cutoffs impose these integer equalities exactly.</p><p>For \\(S=\\sum_jj^2\\alpha_j\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\bigl(\\lambda y_j^2+(\\theta y_j)^TCd_j\\bigr)\n=S\\bigl(\\lambda t^2+(\\theta t)^TCd\\bigr)\n+\\sum_j\\alpha_j(\\theta y_j)^TCk_j.\n\\]\nThe last expression is linear in \\(x,t\\) and separates into linear functions of \\(y_a,y_b\\). Restore the original linear phase likewise.</p><p>The remaining coupled amplitude is smooth on a bounded lift box. Fourier-expand it inside larger product cutoffs. Every term factors in \\(d_a,d_b,d\\), giving atoms with matrices\n\\(-\\alpha_aC,-\\alpha_bC,SC\\).\nApply the same construction to \\(\\phi_k\\), combine horizontal coordinates, and normalize factors. Integration by parts beyond the Fourier dimension by at least \\(110\\) gives \\(DT^{-100}\\). Absolute convergence justifies rearrangement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "Lemma 4.6, Single-scale separation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a coefficient-free separated term from Lemma 3.5 of complexity at most \\(H\\). Let \\(\\zeta\\ge0\\) be smooth, equal to one on the permitted \\(t/r\\) range and supported in a larger fixed interval. Define\n\\[\nA_r=\\sup_\\xi\\left|\\int Q(ru)\\zeta(u)e(-\\xi u)\\,du\\right|.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The resulting bilinear form in the two free normalized \\(L^2(I)\\) slots has norm at most \\(CA_r\\).</p><p>If \\(A_r\\ge\\delta&gt;0\\), its zero-extended kernel on \\(I\\times I\\) has, to uniform error \\(\\varepsilon\\), a finite separated approximation\n\\[\n\\sum_\\nu c_\\nu\\varphi_{\\nu,a}(y_a)\\varphi_{\\nu,b}(y_b),\n\\]\nwith bounded atoms and quasipolynomial complexities, number, and \\(\\sum_\\nu|c_\\nu|\\) in \\(H,\\delta^{-1},\\varepsilon^{-1}\\).\nBefore smooth-kernel separation, retained phases are\n\\[\n\\lambda_*t^2+\\gamma t,\\qquad |\\lambda_*|r^2\\le D,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\lambda_*\\) is a bounded-height rational combination of \\(\\lambda,\\theta\\otimes\\theta\\), and \\(\\gamma-\\beta\\) is such a combination of \\(\\theta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\((y_a,y_b)\\) coordinates, with Jacobian \\(|b-a|\\) and \\(t=(y_b-y_a)/(b-a)\\). Fourier-expand the normalized smooth kernel; each character only modulates the two inputs. Convolution with \\(Q(t)\\zeta(t/r)\\) has multiplier bounded by \\(CrA_r\\). Plancherel and the normalization \\(r^{-2}\\) give the first assertion.</p><p>Apply Lemmas 3.3–3.4 to \\(Q(r\\cdot)\\). They give the retained quadratic and linear phases and the asserted rational dependence. Split\n\\[\ne(\\gamma t)=e(-\\gamma y_a/(b-a))e(\\gamma y_b/(b-a)).\n\\]\nThe remaining kernel \\(w_Ie(\\lambda_*t^2)\\) has controlled normalized derivatives, so its product-cutoff Fourier expansion has quasipolynomial coefficient sum and truncation size. A smooth bump restricted to \\(I\\) is a zero-matrix chart with one additional slow horizontal coordinate, supported inside one lattice cell. Lemma 3.2 realizes the resulting products as atoms. Successive errors are divided by previously bounded coefficient sums.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.7", "orig": "Theorem 4.7, Leng–Sah–Sawhney; external input", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(f:[L]\\to\\mathbb C\\) be \\(1\\)-bounded and \\(\\|f\\|_{U^3[L]}\\ge\\delta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a degree-two filtered nilmanifold \\(G/\\Gamma\\), an adapted polynomial sequence \\(g:\\mathbb Z\\to G\\), and \\(F:G/\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb C\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac1L\\sum_{n=1}^Lf(n)\\overline{F(g(n)\\Gamma)}\\right|\\ge\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nDimension is at most \\(C(1+\\log(1/\\delta))^C\\); nilmanifold complexity, the Lipschitz norm including supremum norm, and \\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\) are at most\n\\(\\exp(C(1+\\log(1/\\delta))^C)\\).\nThere is a rational filtration-adapted Malcev basis with integer second-kind coordinates for \\(\\Gamma\\). No mean-zero hypothesis is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the degree-two instance of the external inverse theorem cited as reference [8, Theorem 1.2], with its stated coordinate and complexity conventions. The paper uses, rather than proves, this result.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.8", "orig": "Lemma 4.8, Elementary cube bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Normalize \\(I\\) to \\([0,1)\\), extend functions by zero to\n\\(\\mathbb T_{16}=\\mathbb R/(16\\mathbb Z)\\), and use probability Haar measure. Define\n\\[\n\\|g\\|_{\\mathcal U}^8\n=\\int_{\\mathbb T_{16}^4}\n\\prod_{\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^3}\\mathcal C^{|\\omega|}\ng(x+\\omega\\cdot h)\\,d\\mu(x)\\,d\\mu(h).\n\\]\nOn an arbitrary interval this norm is denoted \\(\\mathcal U(I)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This is a seminorm. The mixed cube form is bounded by\n\\[\n|\\Lambda_{\\rm cube}(g_\\omega)|\n\\le\\prod_\\omega\\|g_\\omega\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb T_{16})}.\n\\]\nCube forms are continuous in \\(L^2\\), and\n\\(\\|g\\|_{\\mathcal U}\\le C\\|g\\|_{2,[0,1)}\\) for supported functions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The usual three cube Cauchy–Schwarz steps give\n\\[\n|\\Lambda_{\\rm cube}(g_\\omega)|\\le\\prod_\\omega\\|g_\\omega\\|_{\\mathcal U}.\n\\]\nThe same iterations express a single eighth power as an integral of squared absolute values. Expanding the cube for \\(f+g\\) and applying the mixed inequality proves the triangle inequality.</p><p>For the \\(L^2\\) bound, split the vertices into\n\\[\n\\{000,100,010,001\\},\\qquad\n\\{111,011,101,110\\}.\n\\]\nApply Cauchy–Schwarz to the corresponding four-factor groups and change Haar variables in each squared integral; each factors into four squared \\(L^2\\) norms. Telescoping mixed cube differences and truncating prove continuity. Fixed circle-versus-interval normalization gives the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.9", "orig": "Lemma 4.9, Continuous bounded inverse", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(g\\) be supported on \\(I\\), \\(\\|g\\|_\\infty\\le1\\), and\n\\(\\|g\\|_{\\mathcal U(I)}\\ge\\delta\\), \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a bounded atom \\(\\phi\\), of complexity at most\n\\[\nH_\\delta=\\exp(C(1+\\log(1/\\delta))^C),\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\left|\\fint_Ig\\overline\\phi\\right|\\ge H_\\delta^{-1}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize \\(I=[0,1)\\) and set\n\\[\ng_L(j)=L\\int_{j/L}^{(j+1)/L}g(y)\\,dy,\n\\qquad0\\le j&lt;L,\n\\]\nextended by zero to \\(\\mathbb Z/(16L\\mathbb Z)\\). Discrete raw cube averages converge to the continuous one: use Riemann sums for continuous functions and the telescoping bound \\(8\\|g-b\\|_1\\) for \\(1\\)-bounded approximants. There is no wraparound contribution: supported vertices can be lifted into \\([-3,4]\\), where reduction modulo \\(16\\) belongs to \\([0,1)\\) exactly when the real vertex does. Thus, for sufficiently large \\(L\\),\n\\(\\|g_L\\|_{U^3[L]}\\ge c\\delta\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 3.7. In second-kind coordinates write the step-two law as\n\\[\n(z,s)(z',s')=(z+z',s+s'+B(z,z')),\n\\]\nwith rational bounded-height bilinear \\(B\\). The polynomial sequence has\n\\(z(n)=z_0+n\\theta\\) and \\(s(n)\\) an ordinary quadratic polynomial, since its third group differences vanish.</p><p>Insert a nonnegative horizontal Gevrey partition \\(\\sum_m\\rho(z-m)=1\\). Right multiplication by \\((-m,0)\\) represents the nilsequence using\n\\[\n\\rho(z(n)-m)\\widetilde F(z(n)-m,s(n)-B(z(n),m)).\n\\]\nThe central variables are periodic. On a bounded horizontal box the coordinate Lipschitz bound is controlled by the Malcev metric and the group-law formula. Cut off, periodize horizontally, and convolve with product Fejér kernels. A quasipolynomial order gives uniform error at most one quarter of the correlation and a trigonometric polynomial with quasipolynomial coefficient sum.</p><p>For a central frequency \\(k\\), let \\(z^TC_kw=k^TB(z,w)\\). Each summand is\n\\[\ne(k^Ts(n)-z(n)^TC_kz(n))\n\\sum_m\\rho(z(n)-m)e(\\ell^T(z(n)-m)/P_2)\ne(z(n)^TC_k(z(n)-m)).\n\\]\nLemma 3.2 makes it a bounded chart atom. Therefore one discrete atom \\(a\\) has\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac1L\\sum_jg_L(j)\\overline{a(j)}\\right|\\ge\\kappa,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\kappa^{-1}\\) quasipolynomial in \\(\\delta^{-1}\\).</p><p>To interpolate exactly, clear \\(C\\) by \\(P\\), reduce \\(\\theta\\) modulo \\(P\\mathbb Z^d\\), reindex at integer arguments, and reduce the resulting ordinary quadratic coefficient and \\(\\beta\\) modulo one. Thus sampled values have a representation with \\(0\\le\\lambda,\\beta&lt;1\\) and \\(0\\le\\theta_j&lt;P\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\alpha=2\\lambda+\\theta^TC\\theta,\\qquad\n\\Theta=(\\theta,1,\\alpha)^T,\\qquad\n\\widetilde C=\n\\begin{pmatrix}C&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nChoose a Gevrey cutoff \\(\\eta\\) supported in \\((0,1)\\), equal to one except in strips of total length at most \\(2\\epsilon\\), and a partition \\(\\sum_\\ell\\rho_0(r-\\ell)=1\\). Use amplitude\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\chi(w,h,r)\n=\\chi(w-\\theta h)\\eta(h)\\rho_0(r)\ne(-h\\theta^TCw+(\\lambda+\\theta^TC\\theta)h^2-\\beta h).\n\\]\nThe enlarged atom satisfies exactly\n\\[\nA(v)=\\eta(\\{v\\})a(\\lfloor v\\rfloor).\n\\]\nThis follows by expanding its phase and obtaining\n\\(\\lambda m^2+\\beta m+(\\theta m)^TC(\\theta m-n)\\), \\(m=\\lfloor v\\rfloor\\).\nAll amplitude parameters are now controlled and \\(|A|\\le1\\).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\int_0^1g(y)\\overline{a(\\lfloor Ly\\rfloor)}\\,dy\n=\\frac1L\\sum_jg_L(j)\\overline{a(j)}.\n\\]\nReplacing by \\(A(Ly)\\) costs at most \\(2\\epsilon\\). Choose \\(\\epsilon\\le\\kappa/8\\). Rescaling affects only unrestricted real parameters; Lemma 3.2 transfers back to \\(I\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.10", "orig": "Lemma 4.10, Structured, uniform, and \\(L^1\\) decomposition", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(0&lt;\\tau&lt;1/2\\), \\(f\\) on \\(I\\), and \\(\\|f\\|_\\infty\\le1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite decomposition\n\\[\nf=\\sum_\\nu c_\\nu\\phi_\\nu+u+v\n\\]\non \\(I\\), where bounded atoms have complexity at most \\(D_\\tau\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_\\nu|c_\\nu|\\le D_\\tau,\\quad\n\\|u\\|_{\\mathcal U(I)}\\le\\tau,\\quad\n\\|v\\|_{1,I}\\le\\tau,\\quad u,v\\in L^2(I),\n\\]\nand \\(D_\\tau\\le\\exp(C(1+\\log(1/\\tau))^C)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\sigma=\\tau/2\\). Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be the atoms supplied by Lemma 3.9 at threshold \\(\\sigma^2\\), with correlation \\(\\kappa\\). Choose quasipolynomial \\(M\\ge\\max(1,2\\sigma/\\kappa)\\). Consider\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=M\\operatorname{absconv}(\\mathcal A)\n+\\{u\\in L^2:\\|u\\|_{\\mathcal U}\\le\\sigma\\}\n+\\{v\\in L^2:\\|v\\|_1\\le\\sigma\\}.\n\\]\nIt contains an \\(L^2\\) ball.</p><p>If \\(f\\notin\\overline{\\mathcal B}^{L^2}\\), real Hahn–Banach and Riesz give \\(h\\in L^2\\) with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\langle f,h\\rangle&gt;1,\\qquad\n\\sup_{z\\in\\mathcal B}\\operatorname{Re}\\langle z,h\\rangle\\le1.\n\\]\nBalancedness gives\n\\[\n\\|h\\|_\\infty\\le\\sigma^{-1},\\quad\n|\\langle u,h\\rangle|\\le\\sigma^{-1}\\|u\\|_{\\mathcal U},\\quad\n\\sup_{\\phi\\in\\mathcal A}|\\langle h,\\phi\\rangle|\\le M^{-1}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|h\\|_2&gt;1\\), testing with \\(u=h\\) yields\n\\(\\|h\\|_{\\mathcal U}\\ge\\sigma\\|h\\|_2^2&gt;\\sigma\\).\nThen \\(\\sigma h\\) is \\(1\\)-bounded with uniformity norm greater than \\(\\sigma^2\\), contradicting Lemma 3.9 and the atom pairing bound.</p><p>Approximate \\(f\\) from \\(\\mathcal B\\) within \\(\\tau/2\\) in \\(L^2\\) and put that approximation error into the \\(L^1\\) term. This gives a finite structured combination and the stated bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.11", "orig": "Lemma 4.11, Generalized von Neumann bound with one \\(L^2\\) input", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Functions \\(R,g,b,c\\) occupy four distinct slots.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(R,g\\in L^2(I)\\),\n\\[\n|H_I(R,g,b,c)|\n\\le C\\|R\\|_{2,I}\\|g\\|_{\\mathcal U(I)}\n\\|b\\|_{\\infty,I}\\|c\\|_{\\infty,I}.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n|H_I(R,g,b,c)|\n\\le C\\|R\\|_{1,I}\\|g\\|_{1,I}\n\\|b\\|_{\\infty,I}\\|c\\|_{\\infty,I}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize \\(I\\). Extend the kernel smoothly to a fixed periodic box; its Fourier coefficient sum is controlled. For a character-weighted progression average, disintegrate along the coordinate occupied by \\(R\\) and apply Cauchy–Schwarz. Translate in its free direction to introduce \\(h_1\\). Repeat along the \\(b\\)- and \\(c\\)-coordinates, removing their repeated factors at supremum cost and introducing \\(h_2,h_3\\).</p><p>If \\(g\\) occupies slot \\(i\\), and \\(R,b,c\\) occupy \\(j,k,\\ell\\), the remaining eighth-power expression is\n\\[\n\\|R\\|_2^8\\|b\\|_\\infty^8\\|c\\|_\\infty^8\n\\int\\prod_\\omega\\mathcal C^{|\\omega|}\ng(y_i+\\omega_1(i-j)h_1+\\omega_2(i-k)h_2+\\omega_3(i-\\ell)h_3).\n\\]\nThe three nonzero integer multipliers preserve Haar probability, giving \\(\\|g\\|_{\\mathcal U}^8\\). Fourier characters distribute among slots and preserve these norms. Sum the kernel series and extend by \\(L^2\\) approximation using Lemma 3.8.</p><p>For the second bound, use the two coordinates occupied by \\(R,g\\), bound the other factors and kernel in supremum norm, and factor the integral into two \\(L^1\\) norms.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, Delayed predictor replacement", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix two low bounded atoms on a common birth subtree \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In a remaining slot, an admissible sequence associated with \\(F\\in L^2(J;\\mathcal H)\\) is: \\(F\\); a current inherited column projection \\(P_IF\\), with budget \\(D_h\\); or an energy sequence satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{I\\subset J}|I|\\|Z_I\\|_{2,I}^2\\le L^2\\|1_JF\\|_2^2.\n  \\]\n  Fixed finite sums of these are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the finite predictor terms furnished by Lemma 4.2 below. For a term with multiplier \\(\\psi_a\\), its augmented predictor is\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_p=(\\overline{\\psi_a},\\sqrt\\epsilon e_p),\\qquad0&lt;\\epsilon\\le1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The inherited predictor family contains this column on every use interval and its descendants. Other columns are allowed within budget \\(D_h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A use interval has depth \\(s\\), and \\(K_0\\ge0\\) is an integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Replacing \\(F_a\\) by its projection on the partition at depth \\(s+K_0\\) gives total absolute length-weighted error at most\n\\[\nC\\bigl(2^{-K_0}+(1+K_0)\\sqrt\\epsilon\\bigr)\n\\operatorname{poly}(D_{\\rm pred},\\log(2D_h),1+L)\n\\|1_JF_a\\|_2\\|1_JF_b\\|_2.\n\\]\nSequential replacement in both remaining positions is permitted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(E_h\\) be conditional expectation on the depth-\\(h\\) partition and \\(P_h\\) the direct-sum projection. Put\n\\[\nD_h=(f_a-(P_hF_a)_{\\rm phys})\\psi_a,\\qquad\n\\mu_h=E_hD_h.\n\\]\nIf \\(a_{h,p}\\) is the predictor coefficient, Lemma 1.2 gives\n\\(\\mu_h=\\epsilon a_{h,p}\\); thus \\(\\mu_{h+1}-\\mu_h\\) is \\(\\sqrt\\epsilon\\) times a private-coordinate increment.</p><p>For a smooth bump \\(\\omega_I\\), \\(H=s+K_0\\), the exact identity is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_ID_H\\omega_I\n={}&amp;\\int_I\\mu_H\\omega_I\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_{h\\ge H}\\int_I(D_h-D_{h+1})(\\omega_I-E_h\\omega_I)\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_{h\\ge H}\\int_I(\\mu_{h+1}-\\mu_h)\n(E_{h+1}\\omega_I-E_h\\omega_I).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIt follows by telescoping \\(D_h(\\omega_I-E_h\\omega_I)\\); the final remainder vanishes by smooth-bump approximation and uniform \\(L^2\\) boundedness, not by assuming \\(P_hF_a\\to F_a\\).</p><p>Smoothness gives \\(O(2^{s-h})\\) in the two infinite sums. Sum at fixed depth offset and then use orthogonality of \\(P_{h+1}-P_h\\), obtaining aggregate row norm \\(C2^{-K_0}\\|1_JF_a\\|_2\\). For mean-zero \\(\\omega_I\\), replace \\(\\mu_H\\) by \\(\\mu_H-\\mu_s\\); its \\(K_0\\) private increments cost \\(CK_0\\sqrt\\epsilon\\|1_JF_a\\|_2\\).</p><p>Apply the mean-zero tensor decomposition from Lemma 4.1 and pair with the opposing Bessel rows, using Proposition 1 for current projections. For\n\\(w_I(e(\\lambda_*t^2)-1)\\), use a smooth tensor expansion with factor\n\\(|\\lambda_*|r^2\\operatorname{poly}(D_{\\rm pred})\\). Its non-mean-zero initial expectation costs \\(C\\sqrt\\epsilon\\) per depth, summable geometrically in \\(|\\lambda_*|r^2\\).</p><p>A previously delayed projection equals a current projection plus a rolling difference with energy constant \\(L\\le C(1+K_0)\\). It is therefore an admissible opponent in the second replacement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, Scale sum for one separated term", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\psi_a(y_a)\\psi_b(y_b)Q(t)\\) be a fixed separated term of complexity \\(H\\), with all factors bounded by one. Set\n\\[\nA_r=\\sup_\\xi\\left|\\int Q(ru)\\zeta(u)e(-\\xi u)\\,du\\right|,\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathcal I_m\\) contain intervals for which\n\\(e^{-m-1}&lt;A_{|I|}\\le e^{-m}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For admissible sequences in the two remaining positions,\n\\[\n\\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal I_m}|I|\n\\,|H_I(\\psi_az_{a,I},\\psi_bz_{b,I};Q)|\n\\le e^{-m}\\operatorname{poly}(1+m,\\log(2H),\\log(2D_h),1+L)\n\\|1_JF_a\\|_2\\|1_JF_b\\|_2.\n\\]\nIf \\(A_{|I|}=0\\), the corresponding form vanishes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(K=1+m+\\log(2H)\\). Lemmas 3.3–3.6 expand \\(Q\\) into phases\n\\(e(\\lambda_*t^2+\\gamma t)\\) with \\(|\\lambda_*|r^2\\le D_1\\); unscaled frequencies remain bounded-height evaluations of one fixed parameter vector.</p><p>Truncate pooled tails in an absolute scale-sum norm. For each tail pair, write\n\\(e(\\lambda_*t^2)=1+(e(\\lambda_*t^2)-1)\\).\nThe linear part uses the mean-zero square estimate below; the second has smooth-kernel cost\n\\(C|\\lambda_*|r^2\\operatorname{poly}(D_1)\\), summable geometrically over permitted scales. Choose the pooled coefficient tail smaller than \\(e^{-2m}(1+D_1)^{-2}\\), with the required fixed reserve.</p><p>For the finite main catalog, choose a tiny \\(\\varepsilon\\), with logarithm polynomial in \\(K\\). Proposition 2 bounds by \\(\\operatorname{poly}(K)\\) the exceptional scales where a nonzero normalized quadratic or a normalized difference of linear centers lies in an enlarged intermediate annulus. At these scales use the original \\(Ce^{-m}\\) bilinear bound.</p><p>At other scales, bounded effective quadratics are tiny; centers split into tiny-diameter clumps separated by more than \\(\\varepsilon^{-1}/r\\). Proposition 2’s tail bound makes the total error from deleting quadratics and coalescing centers small. Testing \\(Q(r\\cdot)\\) against \\(\\zeta(u)e(-r\\gamma_Cu)\\) shows each combined clump coefficient is \\(O(e^{-m})\\).</p><p>In \\((y_a,y_b)\\) coordinates the kernel has zero integral in each variable. Fourier-expand its compactly supported double primitive and differentiate, obtaining tensors of smooth interior mean-zero bumps. It is enough to estimate rows\n\\[\nW_{I,\\nu}F=|I|^{-1/2}\\int_Ie(\\nu y)\\omega_I(y)F(y)\\,dy,\n\\qquad \\nu=\\pm\\gamma/(b-a).\n\\]\nFor demodulated shells \\(|\\xi+\\nu|\\asymp2^\\ell/|I|\\), their norms are \\(O(2^\\ell)\\) for \\(\\ell&lt;0\\), by mean zero and Poincaré, and \\(O(2^{-2\\ell})\\) for \\(\\ell\\ge0\\), by two antiderivatives. Proposition 2 bounds scale multiplicity for fixed \\(\\xi,\\ell\\) by \\(\\operatorname{poly}(K)\\); separated centers give multiplicity \\(O(1+2^\\ell)\\). Summing square-root multiplicities times shell norms gives a Bessel bound polynomial in \\(K\\). Fixed bounded multipliers preserve it.</p><p>Apply Proposition 1 for current projection sequences and per-interval Bessel for energy sequences. Cauchy–Schwarz between the two row families proves the assertion. If \\(A_r=0\\), Fourier uniqueness makes \\(Q\\zeta(\\cdot/r)\\) vanish.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Corollary 5.2, Finite predictor list", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix two low atoms on \\(J\\), of complexity \\(H_{\\rm low}\\), and \\(B\\ge1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Their product in the form may be replaced by a finite fixed list\n\\[\n\\psi_a(y_a)\\psi_b(y_b)w_I(x,t)e(\\lambda_*t^2),\n\\qquad |\\lambda_*||I|^2\\le D_{\\rm pred},\n\\]\nwith scale-dependent scalar coefficients and error at most\n\\[\ne^{-B}\\operatorname{poly}(\\log(2D_h),1+L)\n\\|1_JF_a\\|_2\\|1_JF_b\\|_2.\n\\]\nList size, coefficient bounds, and atom complexities satisfy\n\\[\n\\log D_{\\rm pred}\\le\\operatorname{poly}(1+B,\\log(2H_{\\rm low})).\n\\]\nThe multipliers may be normalized to supremum at most one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand the low pair by Lemma 3.5 and sum the bands from Lemma 4.1. Discard sufficiently high separation shells and sufficiently small \\(A_r\\)-bands. Their thresholds have logarithms polynomial in the stated parameters. On remaining scales truncate the pooled expansion of Lemma 3.4 in the absolute scale-sum norm used in Lemma 4.1. Distribute \\(e(\\gamma t)\\) between the free slots. The retained rational-height choices form the required fixed list; all discarded errors have the asserted sum.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 5.8, Reduction to the active cell minimum", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work on a finite normalized subtree:\n  \\(\\|f_j\\|_{q,I}\\le1\\) for all its cells.</p><p class=\"li\">Use cyclic update levels \\(t\\), with\n  \\(k_{t+1}=k_t^A\\), where \\(A&gt;1\\) is a sufficiently large absolute constant.</p><p class=\"li\">On bounded-packing inheritance segments, Lemmas 5.1–5.3 below give full tuple arguments \\(Y_l=B_l+U_l\\). Two low roles have \\(U_i=U_j=0\\); the two high roles have rolling differences\n  \\[\n  U_e=(P^{\\rm new}_{e,s+K_0}-P^{\\rm new}_{e,s})F_e,\n  \\qquad e=a,b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Each \\(B_l\\) is a difference of at most two current basic projections. Let\n  \\[\n  e^k\\le D_{\\rm base}\\le\\exp(k^C)\n  \\]\n  dominate path counts, inverse private weights, complexities, and current coefficient sums.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After estimating the term containing both possible \\(U\\)-arguments, remaining patterns have at least three \\(B\\)-arguments and at most one \\(U\\)-argument. They may be restricted, with absolute error at most \\(e^{-k}|R|\\), to a common active collection with path multiplicity\n\\[\nM\\le D_{\\rm base}^C.\n\\]\nLeading update levels have a bound independent of depth. For \\(t\\ge12\\), it suffices, for each retained pattern \\(z\\), to estimate\n\\[\n\\sum_{I\\ {\\rm active}}|I|\\min\\{\\eta,|H_I(z)|\\},\n\\qquad \\eta=e^{-k^\\theta},\n\\]\nfor a fixed absolute \\(\\theta&gt;0\\).</p><p>The coarse nonside arguments are the whole \\(B\\)-arguments before further filters. Restoring them to \\(Y\\) recovers full pair-smallness. Later attempts may restart on normalized subroots while retaining the basic arguments and inherited lists.</p>", "proof": "<p>The rolling differences obey, on every normalized subroot,\n\\[\n\\sum_I|I|\\|U_{e,I}\\|_{2,I}^2\n\\le C(1+K_0)^2|R|,\n\\]\nby telescoping orthogonal increments. The two-\\(U\\) term has complementary full low pair of norm at most \\(\\eta\\), hence absolute sum at most\n\\(C\\eta(1+K_0)^2|R|\\).</p><p>Declare \\(I\\) active if some available triple has transpose norm greater than \\(D_{\\rm base}^{-C'}\\). There are at most \\(D_{\\rm base}^3\\) triples on a path, and Lemma 5.4 gives polynomial logarithmic active-scale count for each, proving \\(M\\le D_{\\rm base}^C\\).</p><p>On inactive cells expand three \\(B\\)-arguments in their original columns. Coefficient sums and the remaining local size cost a fixed power of \\(D_{\\rm base}\\). Lemma 5.4’s small-norm sum and the pathwise triple count make the total at most \\(e^{-k}|R|\\) when \\(C'\\) is sufficiently large. Integrate path bounds using Tonelli.</p><p>For leading levels all budgets are fixed, giving depth-independent bounds. Later full \\(Y\\)-forms have size at most \\(C\\eta\\). Decrease \\(\\theta\\) to absorb the fixed constant. Expand into retained patterns and the estimated two-\\(U\\) term, and use\n\\[\n\\min(\\eta,\\sum_ja_j)\\le\\sum_j\\min(\\eta,a_j).\n\\]\nThe whole \\(B\\)-arguments are retained, so later filter removal restores them exactly; replacing by \\(Y=B+U\\) adds basic sides and restores the full pair estimate. All inherited and rolling estimates restart on normalized subroots.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.4, Inherited adaptive approximation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For a physical residual \\(r\\) in slot \\(j\\), let \\(\\mathfrak d_{j,I}(r)\\) be the supremum of \\(|H_I|\\) against one normalized \\(L^q(I)\\) and two normalized \\(L^2(I)\\) opposing inputs, also taking the supremum over the \\(L^q\\)-slot.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(k\\), on segments with bounded total root length there are inherited projections \\(g_I=P_IF_j\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathfrak d_{j,I}(f_j-(g_I)_{\\rm phys})\\le e^{-k},\n\\qquad\\|g_I\\|_{2,I}\\le1.\n\\]\nPath counts, atom complexities, and physical supremum bounds are at most \\(\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\), with absolute polynomial degrees. Fresh columns may have private weight one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with no columns. At a failed residual test, Proposition 3 gives a bounded atom with correlation at least \\(\\rho\\), where \\(\\log(1/\\rho)\\le k^{O(1)}\\). Insert \\((\\phi,e_v)\\) with a fresh private coordinate and refit. The old residual is zero in that coordinate, so projection energy increases by at least \\(c\\rho^2|I|\\). All fits and refinements form a nested sequence with total gain at most \\(\\|1_JF_j\\|_2^2\\le|J|\\).</p><p>Stop when cumulative insertions would exceed \\(C\\rho^{-2}\\). Integrating insertion counts shows first-excess cells have length at most \\(|J|/4\\) for large \\(C\\). Restart there and use Lemma 1.1. The private-coordinate bound controls coefficient \\(\\ell^2\\) norm by projection norm; Cauchy–Schwarz over the capped catalog gives the physical supremum estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.5, Two-earliest-increments expansion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(g_t\\) be the approximation at update \\(t\\), and \\(g_{t-4}=0\\) before a slot’s first update. For the two earliest increment levels \\(u&lt;t\\) in distinct slots \\(i,j\\), define\n\\[\nX_i=g_u-g_{u-4},\\quad X_j=g_t-g_{t-4},\\quad\nX_a=F_a-g_{a,\\rm prev},\\quad X_b=F_b-g_{b,\\rm prev}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On any finite interval collection, the original form is the limit of the sum of these tuple forms as update cutoff tends to infinity. All tuple arguments have bounded local \\(L^2\\) size. For sufficiently large \\(A\\), some absolute \\(\\theta_0&gt;0\\) gives, for \\(t\\ge12\\), every pair of full tuple arguments bilinear form norm at most\n\\(e^{-2k_t^{\\theta_0}}\\) against normalized \\(L^2\\) opponents.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a finite cutoff, expand each input into increments and terminal residual. In terms with at least two increments, retain the earliest two levels; summing later choices in other slots gives the displayed residual arguments. Terms with at most one increment contain either four terminal residuals or one terminal projection and three residuals.</p><p>For updates \\(h&gt;\\ell\\) in distinct slots, replacing their projections by actual inputs has form-norm error at most\n\\[\nC_q\\bigl(e^{-k_h}\\exp(C_qk_\\ell^d)+e^{-k_\\ell}\\bigr)\n\\]\nfor absolute \\(d\\). Replace the more accurate projection first against the older physical supremum, then the older projection against an actual \\(L^q\\) input. Choosing \\(A&gt;d\\) makes the terminal remainders vanish.</p><p>If \\(u&lt;t-4\\), compare later differences and residuals directly against each early projection; actual-input terms cancel, and later accuracy dominates the early supremum. Other pairs use indices at least \\(t-4\\). If \\(u\\ge t-4\\), distinct slots give \\(u\\ge t-3\\), so all indices are at least \\(t-7\\). Expand pairs into projection/actual pairs; at least one full argument has zero coefficient sum after replacement, so two-actual terms cancel. Errors are \\(O(\\exp(-c_qk_{t-7}))\\); since \\(k_{t-7}=k_t^{A^{-7}}\\), decrease \\(\\theta_0&lt;A^{-7}\\). Contraction gives the \\(L^2\\) bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Proposition 5.6, Truncating the two high positions", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a tuple from Lemma 5.2 and a common older inheritance segment. Put \\(k=k_t\\), and call the two increment slots low.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(K_0\\le k^{O(1)}\\), further bounded-packing segments, and nested high projection families such that\n\\[\nY_i=X_i,\\quad Y_j=X_j,\\quad\nY_e=P^{\\rm new}_{e,s+K_0}F_e-g_{e,\\rm prev}\\quad(e=a,b).\n\\]\nThe total absolute replacement error is at most \\(Ce^{-k}\\) times the original normalized root length. Path counts, inverse private weights, complexities, and coarse-cell physical suprema are at most \\(\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\). All \\(Y\\)-sizes are bounded. For \\(t\\ge12\\), every full pair has form norm at most \\(\\eta=e^{-k^\\theta}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 4.2 to each available low-column pair, at precision whose logarithm is a sufficiently large fixed polynomial in \\(k\\). Admit the conjugate predictors at first joint availability and inherit them. Choose \\(K_0\\) and \\(\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\), after the predictor budget, as sufficiently large fixed polynomials. Proposition 4 and the predictor truncation errors then sum to less than \\(e^{-2k}\\) after low-label coefficients and path counts. Later high-catalog enlargement enters only through a polynomial in its logarithm.</p><p>For pair-birth roots \\(J\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_J\\|1_JF_a\\|_2\\|1_JF_b\\|_2\\le D_{\\rm old}^C|R|,\n\\]\nby Cauchy–Schwarz and path multiplicity. This justifies the low-pair summation. A previously delayed opponent is current plus a rolling energy sequence.</p><p>Add tuple-specific accurate fits chronologically at projection depth \\(h\\), testing residuals on the coarse cell of depth \\(s=h-K_0\\). A failed coarse test supplies an atom with moment \\(\\rho'\\). Insert one common fresh label on every depth-\\(h\\) subcell \\(Q\\subset I\\). If \\(m_Q\\) is its residual moment, the joint gain is\n\\[\nc\\sum_Q|Q||m_Q|^2\n\\ge c|I|\\left|\\fint_I{\\rm residual}\\,\\overline\\phi\\right|^2\n\\ge c(\\rho')^2|I|.\n\\]\nCap cumulative requests at a large multiple of \\((\\rho')^{-2}\\), stop on coarse first-excess cells, and restart. Continue old estimators by inheritance below cuts so previously assigned ancestor summands retain genuine nested extensions.</p><p>Choose the first high precision beyond older and predictor costs; choose the second beyond the first catalog’s supremum bound. On fine \\(Q\\),\n\\[\n\\|F_e\\|_{2,Q}\\le2^{K_0/2}\\|F_e\\|_{2,I},\n\\]\nso this supremum depends only on the first high catalog, not the second precision. The staggered comparison of Lemma 5.2 then gives full-pair smallness. Contraction on fine partitions gives local size bounds. These fits do not alter the global recurrence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.7, Few active scales for a fixed triple", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix three bounded basic scalar columns in distinct slots on their common birth subtree. Let \\(A_I\\) be their form norm against one normalized \\(L^2(I)\\) input.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Along every spatial path,\n\\[\n\\#\\{I:A_I&gt;\\delta\\}\n\\le\\operatorname{poly}(\\log D_{\\rm base}+\\log(1/\\delta)),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{I:A_I\\le\\delta}A_I\n\\le\\delta\\operatorname{poly}(\\log D_{\\rm base},\\log(1/\\delta)).\n\\]\nThe bounds are uniform in unrestricted oscillation parameters and path location.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a path point \\(y\\) and transpose into the fourth slot \\(l\\). Its squared \\(L^2\\) norm is an integral in normalized variables \\((h,t,t')/r\\), containing the triple and its conjugate copy and two kernels. The six atoms form a multivariable chart. Recenter at \\(y\\), fixing all unscaled parameters along the path.</p><p>If \\(A_I&gt;\\delta\\), Lemmas 3.3–3.4 give a finite expansion, accurate relative to \\(\\delta^2\\). Effective quadratic entries and linear \\(t\\)-frequencies are respectively \\(r^2a\\cdot\\chi_*\\) and \\(ra\\cdot\\chi_*\\), with quasipolynomial height and fixed parameter-vector dimension.</p><p>By Proposition 2, outside polynomially many intermediate scales, bounded quadratics are tiny and linear \\(t\\)-frequencies are tiny or large. Delete tiny quadratics. Large frequencies integrate negligibly by smoothness; tiny frequencies may be replaced by constants, after which\n\\[\n\\int w_I(y+h-lt,t)\\,dt=0.\n\\]\nThe other kernel and remaining linear factors are independent of \\(t\\). All errors are below \\(\\delta^2\\), a contradiction. Summing this count over bands\n\\(2^{-n-1}\\delta&lt;A_I\\le2^{-n}\\delta\\) gives the second assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 6.8, Localization into current groups", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an active pattern from Proposition 5 on normalized root \\(R\\), with fixed original basic lists.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(P=\\lceil\\eta^{-\\alpha}\\rceil\\), where \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) is chosen at the final parameter stage.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c=(-1,3,-3,1)\\). An original column in slot \\(i\\) has normalized tag \\(p_v=(\\theta_v,C_v,\\lambda_v)\\) when its phase is\n  \\[\n  c_i\\bigl(\\lambda_vy^2+(\\theta_vy)^TC_v(\\theta_vy-n)\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  Linear modulation is not part of the tag. Original-vertex lists increase along paths and have size at most \\(D=D_{\\rm base}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At depth \\(s\\), tags are adjacent in the ambient graph when their difference data\n  \\[\n  V=r(\\theta_v,\\theta_w)^T,\\quad\n  C=\\operatorname{diag}(C_v,-C_w),\\quad\n  \\Lambda=r^2(\\lambda_v-\\lambda_w)\n  \\]\n  admit the splitting of Lemma 3.3 at threshold \\(H_{\\rm ed}\\). Equal tags have distance zero; write \\(d_s\\) for graph distance. Edges persist under refinement.</p><p class=\"li\">Additional main and helper columns attach to original vertices, use the same normalized tag in each slot, and have bounded template amplitudes and unrestricted linear modulations. They do not create graph vertices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After exceptional-root cuts of arbitrarily small fixed total relative length, there are \\(\\operatorname{poly}(k)\\) fixed search leaves for which, up to total absolute error \\(Ce^{-k}|R|\\), each leaf contributes\n\\[\n\\sum_{G\\ {\\rm current}}\nH_I(Z_{0,G,s},Z_{1,G,s},Z_{2,G,s},Z_{3,G,s}),\n\\qquad\nZ_{l,G,s}=\\overline L_{l,G,s}\\mathcal B_{l,s}z_l.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\overline L\\) is one positive smeared ball filter; \\(\\mathcal B_{l,s}\\) is a product of cumulative filters or complements of length \\(O(\\log L)\\), independent of \\(G\\). Slot and product choices are fixed by the leaf. Groups are used only at actual current central assignments. The original cell minimum may be split separately over leaves.</p><p>All main catalogs have logarithmic budget \\(k^{O(1)}\\), independent of later mode precision. A helper rank may subsequently be closed on the fixed whole side states and their required histories, with the same type of budget.</p>", "proof": "<p>Perform binary search on colors, maintaining an upper primal guard and, when needed, a lower residual guard. At a midpoint \\(m\\), split a third slot by its cumulative smeared filter and complement. The positive term becomes the upper guard on \\([\\ell,m]\\); the residual term becomes the lower guard on \\([m+1,h]\\). Release the former corresponding guard and do not postcompose a guard before release. Use deterministic slot choices. There are \\(O(\\log L)\\) splits and at most \\(L\\) leaves.</p><p>At a final color \\(k'\\), expand only the upper guard’s outer primal support. Call a tag old if it is within the upper pad of a vertex of color at most \\(k'-1\\). The untouched lower residual guard has larger rank and a pad margin of at least \\(10P\\), so Lemma 6.6 kills old sources. For \\(k'=1\\), none occur.</p><p>Each remaining source is near a current vertex of color \\(k'\\). Lemma 6.3 places it in the first ball masks of its unique group, separated from every other same-color group. Insert a third slot’s first-rank ball projection summed over groups. For the matching group use closure; for other groups expand the new primal and use Lemma 6.1. Keep the lower guard untouched for old terms.</p><p>Use this third-slot primal source to insert the larger-rank, larger-pad ball filters in the other three slots. Closure bounds every residual insertion. This gives the stated group form with shared cumulative histories.</p><p>When testing a rank-\\(r\\) residual, the expanded lower source has coefficient sum at most\n\\(D\\sqrt{n_{r-1}}\\) times its input size, canceled by the closure tolerance. Counts of cells, leaves, groups, and steps are fixed powers of \\(D\\); choose \\(C_\\tau\\) large. Pair-mismatch errors may additionally pay main/helper counts, already anticipated before choosing \\(\\tau_2\\). Their total is \\(Ce^{-k}|R|\\). No full coefficient expansion remains in the main identity.</p><p>Apply \\(\\min(\\eta,\\sum a_j)\\le\\sum\\min(\\eta,a_j)\\) over leaves. Proposition 1 bounds shared coarse histories by the small overhead\n\\[\nG_0=\\exp(\\log^{O(1)}(2k)).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemmas 6.1 and 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Single-column complexities are bounded by \\(\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\). Choose \\(\\tau_2&gt;0\\) with \\(\\log(1/\\tau_2)\\le k^{O(1)}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One may choose \\(H_{\\rm ed}=\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\) so that \\(d_s(v,w)&gt;1\\) implies:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. same-slot normalized physical inner products are at most \\(\\tau_2\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. distinct-slot pair form norms against two normalized \\(L^2\\) spectators are at most \\(\\tau_2\\).</p><p>If two tags are joined at one scale by a path of length at most \\(\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\), their endpoint difference data satisfy Lemma 3.3’s splitting with \\(\\log H'\\le\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For the same-slot assertion, smooth the interval indicator with \\(L^1\\) error much below \\(\\tau_2\\). A larger inner product gives a large Fourier coefficient of the conjugate chart product, and Lemma 3.3 supplies an edge.</p><p>For distinct source slots \\(i,j\\), express them in spectator coordinates \\(a,b\\):\n\\[\ny_l=r(\\xi_lz+\\zeta_lw)+\\text{constant},\\quad\n\\xi_l=(b-l)/(b-a),\\quad\\zeta_l=(l-a)/(b-a).\n\\]\nThe cancellation identities give\n\\(c_i\\xi_i\\zeta_i=-c_j\\xi_j\\zeta_j=\\kappa\\ne0\\).\nClip spectators at inverse-polynomial height in \\(\\tau_2\\); Lemma 3.1 bounds discarded tails. Apply the small-return calculation of Lemma 3.3 to the difference data. Translation produces modulation \\(\\kappa\\Omega(h,m)\\) in \\(w\\), with the \\(z\\)-modulation absorbed into the other spectator.</p><p>A correlation above \\(\\tau_2\\), repeated over many small-return multiples, would pair one fixed bounded kernel with tests \\(f_\\ell(z)g(w)e(\\ell\\kappa\\Omega w)\\). If \\(|\\Omega|\\) exceeded a fixed constant, these modulated bounded tests would be Bessel, contradicting the sum of their large squared correlations. Thus small returns have bounded \\(\\Omega\\), and the algebraic successive-minima part of Lemma 3.3 gives an edge.</p><p>For the endpoint assertion, concatenate all horizontal path coordinates. Impose the rational equations requiring each consecutive pair to lie in its edge fast space. Determinant bounds control the projection onto the joint solution space and a right inverse, so the concatenated horizontal vector is within a controlled error of it. Replacing an edge’s fast vector by the joint one changes its curvature correction only through bounded slow vectors, by isotropy. Summing edge effective quadratics telescopes to endpoint data. The endpoint image of the joint fast space is rational and isotropic; projecting onto it changes the correction by another controlled isotropic error.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.3, Color budget and exceptional roots", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Set\n\\[\nd_0=\\lceil1000\\log(2D)\\rceil,\\quad L=d_0^{30},\\quad\nH_{\\max}=100P(L+1),\n\\]\n\\[\nb_2=100(H_{\\max}+1),\\qquad\nb_1=d_0^{10}b_2(H_{\\max}+1).\n\\]\nIn each of \\(d_0\\) primary rounds assign independent geometric delays, truncated to \\([0,d_0]\\), with untruncated tail \\(2^{-n}\\). Score \\(z\\) at \\(v\\) by\n\\(D_z-\\lfloor d_s(z,v)/b_1\\rfloor\\).\nUse the first round with a unique winner separated by at least five.</p><p>For winner admission index \\(i\\) and current list size \\(j\\), use an equality cohort if \\(i=j\\); otherwise use the smallest dyadic index block whose lower half contains \\(i\\) and upper half contains \\(j\\), freezing \\(W\\) to the lower-half prefix. For primary score \\(g\\), define\n\\[\nf_a(s')=\\max_{w\\in W\\setminus\\{z\\}}(b_1D_w-d_{s'}(w,a)),\n\\]\n\\[\nT_l=(g-3)b_1+\\frac{lb_1}{2d_0},\\quad U=(g-2)b_1,\\quad\nC_l(x)=\\min(U,\\max(T_l,x)),\n\\]\n\\[\nd^{(l)}(a,b)=\\sup_{s'\\in\\mathbb Z}|C_l(f_a(s'))-C_l(f_b(s'))|.\n\\]\nPut \\(R_l=10l(d_0+1)b_2\\). Choose the first \\(l&lt;d_0\\) where the \\(d^{(l)}\\)-ball of radius \\(R_l\\) and \\(d^{(l+1)}\\)-ball of radius \\(R_{l+1}\\) have the same floor logarithm to base \\(3/2\\), denoted \\(n\\). Use independent high geometric rounds with metric \\(d^{(l+1)}/b_2\\), again requiring a gap of five.</p><p>Color metadata record the selected rounds and scores, cohort level, \\(l,n\\), and a change counter initialized at \\(d_0^8\\), decremented whenever data or winning identities change. A group additionally records winners, frozen prefix, and exact cohort block.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The construction uses at most \\(L\\) ordered colors, nonincreasing along paths, with at most \\(d_0^8\\) changes per vertex. Delays can be chosen so that first failures of required markings have arbitrarily small fixed total relative root length.</p>", "proof": "<p>Competitor scores only increase. At fixed top score, a marked winner can occur in only one episode: once a rival destroys its gap, that rival cannot decrease. Thus primary data have at most \\(5d_0^2\\) episodes. Each has at most \\(d_0\\) cohorts; within a cohort the fixed-metric ball log-size vector has at most \\(2d_0^2\\) epochs; each supports at most \\(5d_0^2\\) high-data episodes. The total is at most \\(50d_0^7\\le d_0^8\\). Seven metadata coordinates and the counter fit in fewer than \\(256d_0^{15}&lt;L\\) states. Counter-first ordering makes colors nonincreasing.</p><p>In an untruncated score situation, increase the canonical winner’s delay by five. This injects all outcomes into marked outcomes with probability ratio \\(2^{-5}\\). Truncation loses at most \\(D2^{-d_0}\\), leaving marking probability at least \\(1/64\\).</p><p>Along a path only polynomially many situations occur: cap irrelevant pair distances at \\(d_0+5\\), use their monotonicity, and count primary, cohort, and ball epochs. Conditional on primary arrays, high situations are fixed before independent high arrays are revealed. A union bound with failure probability \\((1-1/64)^{d_0}\\), then integration over the root, gives a realization with small failed-path measure. First failure cells are disjoint and have at most that measure.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, Mask inclusion, closure, and separation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For a successful group \\(G\\), pad \\(1\\le h\\le H_{\\max}\\), and historical central witness \\(v_0\\) of that group and color, include a present vertex \\(b\\) when\n\\[\nb_1D_z-d_s(z,b)\\ge(g-1)b_1-h,\\qquad\nd^{(l+1)}(b,v_0)\\le h,\\qquad\nd^{(l)}(b,v_0)\\le10R_lh.\n\\]\nThe union over historical witnesses is \\(M_{G,h}\\). It is inherited after its cohort when needed for nested operators. An actual use has a current center assigned to \\(G\\). A usable endpoint has a nonempty mask and an actual use at or below it.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. At an actual use centered at \\(v\\), every present \\(b\\) with \\(d_s(b,v)\\le h\\) lies in \\(M_{G,h}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At an actual use, graph neighbors of pad-\\(h\\) vertices lie in pad \\(h+1\\). Every admitted mask vertex has a path to \\(z\\) of length \\(\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Distinct same-color groups have graph distance greater than one between vertices in their masks on overlapping usable endpoints, measured at the finer endpoint.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a marked center, rivals have true score at most \\((g-5)b_1\\), while the winner’s true score exceeds \\((g-1)b_1\\). Earlier rival profiles lie below clipping thresholds. A vertex within distance \\(h\\) loses at most \\(h\\) in winner score and has clipped profile distance at most \\(h\\) at all later depths. This proves inclusion.</p><p>Historical and current centers share the high winner, hence are within \\(2(d_0+1)b_2\\) in the high metric. Their low balls have at least \\((3/2)^n\\) points and both lie in a high ball with fewer than \\((3/2)^{n+1}\\); they intersect. Thus their low-metric distance is at most \\(2R_l\\). A pad vertex has low distance at most\n\\(A_h=10R_lh+2R_l\\) from the current center. The numerical choices give \\(A_h&lt;T_{l+1}-T_l-10\\), so its true rival profile is below \\(T_{l+1}-10\\). A graph neighbor costs one, establishing both profile predicates at pad \\(h+1\\). The winner-score predicate gives the short path at admission.</p><p>For separation, take overlapping endpoints and a downstream actual-use certificate for the later group. If primary winners differ, the earlier winner belongs to the later frozen prefix at the common cohort level; its persistent score contradicts the later rival bound. If primary winners agree, the cohort and metrics agree, so distinct groups have different high winners. At the later historical center, the earlier high winner’s score has fallen by more than \\(4b_2\\), forcing historical-center distance greater than \\(4b_2\\). Adjacency of mask vertices, the true rival bound, and persistence imply their high profile distance is at most one, hence historical-center distance at most \\(2H_{\\max}+1\\), a contradiction. This also covers certificates on different branches.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Lemma 6.5, Bessel separation of usable endpoint spaces", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a slot and color. For each group \\(G\\), let \\(Z_G\\) be a finite sum of available main columns with constant coefficients on entire usable endpoint cells whose masks contain their tags. Repetitions and unequal endpoint times are allowed. Group counts, label path counts, and inverse private weights are bounded by \\(\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently small \\(\\tau_2\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_GZ_G\\right\\|_2^2\\lesssim\\sum_G\\|Z_G\\|_2^2,\n\\]\nindependently of repeated endpoint times and future-certificate depths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Combine repeated occurrences of a label into\n\\(a_{v,G}(y)=\\sum_Ia_{v,G,I}1_I(y)\\).\nPrivate coordinates give\n\\(\\sum_v|a_{v,G}(y)|^2\\le W_0^2\\|Z_G(y)\\|^2\\).</p><p>For two labels in distinct groups, graph separation holds up to a deterministic last depth \\(s_*\\). By Lemma 6.3, overlapping supporting endpoints intersect only at depths at most \\(s_*\\). Therefore\n\\(a_{v,G}\\overline{a_{w,G'}}\\) is measurable on the depth-\\(s_*\\) grid. Apply Lemma 6.1 on its cells:\n\\[\n|\\langle Z_G,Z_{G'}\\rangle|\n\\le\\tau_2\\sum_{v,w}\\int|a_{v,G}a_{w,G'}|.\n\\]\nPointwise Cauchy–Schwarz over groups and labels bounds all cross terms by \\(\\tau_2\\) times a fixed power of the path and private-weight budgets times \\(\\sum_G\\|Z_G\\|_2^2\\). Choose \\(\\tau_2\\) accordingly.</p><p>Whole-cell ranges are essential: a restriction of an output moves to the input side of the endpoint projection under adjunction, and that projection again outputs constant coefficients on its entire endpoint cell.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "Lemma 6.6, Uniform same-tag test", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For each original vertex \\(v\\), use bounded templates of its normalized tag in every slot, with unrestricted linear modulations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If a pure source column of tag \\(v\\) in slot \\(i\\) has form norm \\(t_0&gt;0\\) against a physical residual in slot \\(j\\) and normalized \\(L^2\\) spectators, the residual has a moment at least \\(t_0/H_0\\) against a same-tag template in slot \\(j\\), with \\(H_0\\le\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\). Source columns may themselves be templates. The bound is independent of insertion rank and linear modulation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a nonnegative partition \\(\\rho\\), an integer period \\(P_0\\) accommodating fixed supports, and amplitudes\n\\[\n\\rho(d)e(\\ell^Td/P_0),\\qquad d=\\theta_vy-n,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\ell\\) reduced modulo \\(P_0\\), together with arbitrary external linear modulation.</p><p>Insert partitions in the other slots and extract matching phases. Lifts satisfy\n\\(d_l=(1-l)d_0+ld_1+k_l\\) with bounded integer carries. The identities\n\\(\\sum_lc_ll^u=0\\), \\(u=0,1,2\\), leave only carry-dependent linear phases. Impose carry equalities with smooth equality cutoffs and Fourier-expand on the bounded lift box. Reduce indices by\n\\[\ne(\\ell^Td/P_0)=e(q^T\\theta_vy)e(r^Td/P_0),\n\\qquad\\ell=P_0q+r.\n\\]\nThus nonlinear template complexity does not iterate. Absolute Fourier sums are bounded by \\(H_0\\).</p><p>For a separated term, express the source and target coordinates through the spectator coordinates and Fourier-expand the smooth kernel. Fourier coefficients of the demodulated target are its same-tag moments. If their supremum is \\(M\\), the resulting sum is bounded by\n\\[\nM\\sum_m|A_mB_m|\n\\le M\\Bigl(\\sum_m|A_m|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2}\n       \\Bigl(\\sum_m|B_m|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe nonzero spectator frequency spacings give Bessel bounds. Sum the absolutely convergent separated terms to obtain form norm at most \\(H_0M\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.6", "orig": "Lemma 6.7, Catalog closure and budget", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Define cumulative masks\n\\[\nU_{m,h}(I)=\\{v:d_s(v,\\{w:\\operatorname{color}(w)\\le m\\})\\le h\\}.\n\\]\nOriginal columns have rank zero. A rank-\\(r\\) projection uses available columns of ranks at most \\(r\\) with tags in its mask. A positive smeared filter averages \\(P\\) pad projections.</p><p>At split \\(m\\), use rank \\(L-m+1\\) and base pad \\(10P(L-m+1)\\). Final ball filters have ranks \\(L+j\\), \\(1\\le j\\le4\\), and base pads\n\\(20P(L+1)+10(j-1)P\\).</p><p>For all required histories of length \\(O(\\log L)\\), impose at target rank \\(r\\)\n\\[\n\\text{residual form norm}\\le\n\\frac{\\tau_3}{D\\sqrt{n_{r-1}}}\\|\\text{base}\\|_{2,I},\n\\qquad\\tau_3=D^{-C_\\tau},\n\\]\nagainst every lower-rank pure source in the mask and two arbitrary normalized \\(L^2\\) spectators. Include all individual pad choices and complements. Helper rank \\(L+5\\) is closed later on fixed whole side states, with \\(\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\) bases per active cell.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Main closure and subsequent helper closure terminate with\n\\(\\log n_{\\max}\\le k^{O(1)}\\).\nThe exponent is independent of \\(H_{\\rm ed}\\) and later linearization precision. Fresh columns have private weight one and the fixed template complexities of Lemma 6.5.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a failed test, Lemma 6.5 supplies a fresh template with moment at least the threshold divided by \\(H_0\\). Its new private coordinate is zero in the old state, so the target projection increment applied to the old history has norm at least\n\\[\n\\frac{c\\tau_3}{D\\sqrt{n_{r-1}}H_0}\\|\\text{base}\\|_{2,I}.\n\\]\nFor a named history and trial sequence of \\(T\\) insertions, every positive projection is nested in insertion time. Expand complements and apply the binary-prefix row argument of Proposition 1 to target increments and old-index histories. Total squared action is at most\n\\[\n(C\\log(2T))^{O(\\log L)}\\|\\text{base}\\|_{2,I}^2.\n\\]\nThis charges a changing history without treating it as fixed.</p><p>Numbers of named masks and histories are \\(\\exp(k^{O(1)})\\). The bound for rank-\\(r\\) insertions is\n\\(n_{r-1}\\exp(k^{C_1})(C\\log(2T))^{O(\\log L)}\\), with no extra count of triggering sources. Absorb active path multiplicity and choose\n\\(n_r/n_{r-1}=\\exp(k^{C_2})\\).\nThere are only \\(k^{O(1)}\\) ranks, so the resulting global trial cap has logarithm \\(k^{O(1)}\\), and the prefix factor is below the reserved ratio. No cap is attained. The same argument closes helpers after their bases are fixed.</p><p>Counts depend on named contexts, not numeric graph distances. Therefore fix counts first, choose \\(\\tau_2\\) after them, and choose \\(H_{\\rm ed}\\) last via Lemma 6.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 6.9, Outgoing filters and near-band defects", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>At a localized use, let slots \\(a,b\\) contain fixed whole side states \\(S_a,S_b\\), chosen independently of helper insertion. Slots \\(c,d\\) contain whole localized coarse arguments based on \\(B_c,B_d\\).</p><p>For an outgoing filter on \\(c\\), let \\(Q_{c,p}\\), \\(1\\le p\\le P\\), be its individual projections and \\(Q_{a,p}\\) the corresponding helper projections. Set\n\\[\nQ_{l,0}=0,\\quad Q_{l,P+1}=1,\\quad\nA_l^p=Q_{l,p}-Q_{l,p-1},\\quad\n\\overline Q_l=P^{-1}\\sum_{p=1}^PQ_{l,p}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Expand complementary factors \\(1-\\overline Q\\) in slots \\(c,d\\). All resulting positive filters can be removed successively, transferring corresponding helper averages to slot \\(a\\). The final main term is\n\\[\nH_I(D_{a,s}S_a,S_b,B_c,B_d),\n\\]\nwhere \\(D_{a,s}\\) has length \\(O(\\log L)\\).</p><p>At a transfer step, with inner outgoing history \\(x_c\\), already-transferred side \\(x_a\\), other side \\(x_b=S_b\\), and remaining whole complement \\(x_d\\), the exact defect is\n\\[\nH_I(x_a,x_b,\\overline Q_cx_c,x_d)\n-H_I(\\overline Q_ax_a,x_b,x_c,x_d)\n=\n\\sum_{p,q=1}^{P+1}\\frac{p-q}{P}\nH_I(A_a^px_a,x_b,A_c^qx_c,x_d).\n\\]\nOnly \\(|p-q|\\le2\\) need be retained. The far terms have size at most\n\\(D^{-C_3}\\|S_a\\|_{2,I}\\|S_b\\|_{2,I}\\) for any prescribed fixed \\(C_3\\), after the paper’s accuracy choices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand complements, costing \\(2^{O(\\log L)}=\\operatorname{poly}(k)\\). Remove the larger rank among current outermost filters on \\(c,d\\); the other primal source has strictly smaller rank. Band \\(p\\) has average weight \\((P-p+1)/P\\), including weight zero on the terminal band, which proves the exact identity.</p><p>If \\(p&gt;q+2\\), expand the lower \\(c\\)-band into main primals. Their tags belong to both helper masks defining the higher residual \\(a\\)-band, so helper closure bounds it.</p><p>If \\(q&gt;p+2\\), expand the lower-rank primal source of \\(x_d\\). A source within one graph edge of the lower helper mask belongs, by neighbor closure, to both masks of the higher \\(c\\)-residual. Main closure applies, with the entire helper band as an \\(L^2\\) spectator. A far source is separated from every helper primal; expand that primal and apply Lemma 6.1. The two-pad gap provides the neighbor margin. Thus helper insertions do not change main closure.</p><p>Closure denominators cancel lower-source coefficient sums. Pair mismatch may pay full counts, absorbed by the earlier \\(\\tau_2\\). Sum \\(O(P^2)\\) pairs and polynomially many steps. Retain near-band sums together at each fixed offset and transfer only the main term to the next step.</p><p>For defect estimation, split the terminal complementary ball band\n\\(1-Q_{c,P}\\) into identity and positive ball parts. All other ball endpoints have usable ranges. Once an outgoing filter is cumulative, its outer ball has been removed and its band stack is shared across groups; the other complement is either outer-ball or shared. This avoids duplicating shared histories.</p><p>Finally, replacing \\(B_c,B_d\\) by \\(Y_c,Y_d\\) in the main term introduces an additional basic \\(U\\)-side; the remaining full pair has the smallness from Proposition 5.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, Short-block comparison", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(2&lt;q&lt;3\\). A finite dyadic tree piece has root \\(R\\), internal summation intervals in at most \\(K\\) consecutive depths within the original \\(N\\)-depth range, and terminal leaves in that block or one depth below it. Deterministic Hilbert-valued inputs satisfy\n\\(\\|z_j\\|_{2,L}\\le s_j\\) on every leaf \\(L\\). Random scalar inputs satisfy every fixed moment bound used in the proof,\n\\[\n(\\mathbb E_\\sigma|z_j^\\sigma(y)|^a)^{1/a}\n\\le C_a\\|z_j(y)\\|.\n\\]\nConstants are independent of the tree and \\(K\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For arbitrary measurable \\(|\\epsilon_I(\\sigma)|\\le1\\) and any internal subcollection,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left|\n\\sum_I|I|\\epsilon_I(\\sigma)\nH_I(z_0^\\sigma,z_1^\\sigma,z_2^\\sigma,z_3^\\sigma)\n\\right|\n\\le\n(1+C_N(q))C_q(1+\\log(K+2))^C\n(K+2)^{C(q-2)}|R|\\prod_js_j.\n\\]\nProducts of at most two independent Rademacher randomizations of a vector stack satisfy the required moment hypothesis.</p>", "proof": "<p>By homogeneity assume \\(s_j=1\\). Absorb a common random unimodular scalar into kernel signs, reducing to the absolute value of an expected signed sum.</p><p>Partition four-tuples of leaf depths into rectangles. If the minimum occurs in at least two slots, specify all attaining slots and designate two. If the minimum is unique in slot \\(a\\), use the smallest dyadic index interval separating it from the second minimum; designate a slot \\(b\\) in the upper half and require remaining slots to lie there or above it as specified. At each of \\(O(1+\\log(K+2))\\) length levels, the designated masks \\(E_{a,\\iota}\\) and \\(E_{b,\\iota}\\) are separately disjoint over positions \\(\\iota\\). There are \\(O(K+1)\\) positions.</p><p>For one rectangle set \\(t\\) to its upper-half threshold. All slots are regular, with leaf depth at least \\(t\\), except possibly the unique minimum slot, called heavy. Summation depths satisfy \\(s&lt;t\\): a selected leaf of depth at most \\(s\\) cannot meet the interior of an internal interval at depth \\(s\\).</p><p>Take \\(\\delta=\\beta r_t\\) with fixed small dyadic \\(\\beta\\), and write\n\\[\ny_0=\\delta(n_0+u),\\quad y_1=\\delta(n_1+v),\\quad\nn_j=(1-j)n_0+jn_1,\\quad w_j=(1-j)u+jv.\n\\]\nOn a fixed large torus, define\n\\(F_j^\\sigma(n,w)=z_j^\\sigma(\\delta(n+w))1_{E_j}(\\delta(n+w))\\),\nusing \\([0,1)\\) in slots \\(0,1\\) and fixed bounded lifts in slots \\(2,3\\). The fast Haar progression integral reproduces the original one.</p><p>Sample the kernel at \\((\\delta n_0,\\delta(n_1-n_0))\\). Error is \\(O(\\delta/r_s)\\). Pair-coordinate absolute estimates hold on lattice counting measure times the connected torus. If \\(M_{j,I}\\) are deterministic squared masses of relevant windows, the support margin gives\n\\[\nM_{j,I}\\le C|I|,\\qquad\n\\sum_{\\operatorname{depth}I=s}M_{j,I}\\le C|E_j|.\n\\]\nExpected errors at depth \\(s\\) sum to at most\n\\(C(\\delta/r_s)|E_a|^{1/2}|E_b|^{1/2}\\).\nTheir geometric depth sum and designated disjointness cost \\(C|R|\\) per length level.</p><p>On fast Fourier coefficients use\n\\[\n\\widehat{U_{j,\\gamma}F}(n,m)\n=e(\\gamma m^2/c_j)\\widehat F(n,m),\n\\qquad \\gamma\\in\\mathbb R/(3\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nThe progression integral is unchanged: its indices satisfy\n\\(\\sum_jm_j=\\sum_jjm_j=0\\), hence\n\\(m_j=c_j(h+jh')\\) and \\(\\sum_jm_j^2/c_j=0\\).\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\gamma\\int|U_{j,\\gamma}F_j^\\sigma(n,w)|^4\\,dw\n\\le2\\|F_j^\\sigma(n,\\cdot)\\|_2^4.\n\\]\nIntegration equates sums and sums of squares of two Fourier indices, forcing equal multisets. Fourier-polynomial approximation and the same fourth-moment bound justify \\(L^2\\) limits.</p><p>Let \\(e_j(n)\\) be deterministic fast-window \\(L^2\\) size. Then\n\\[\n\\delta\\sum_ne_j(n)^2\\le C|E_j|.\n\\]\nFor regular slots \\(e_j(n)\\le C\\), because all meeting leaves have length at most \\(r_t\\) and total length at most the window length plus \\(2r_t\\). In all slots,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta}{|I|}\\sum_{n\\ {\\rm relevant\\ to}\\ I}e_j(n)^2\\le C.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(G_j=U_{j,\\gamma}F_j^\\sigma\\). Clip a heavy slot radially at \\(A=(K+2)^{10}\\). By unitarity,\n\\(\\|G_a-G_a^A\\|_1\\le A^{-1}\\|F_a^\\sigma\\|_2^2\\).\nThe eighth sign moment and fourth fast moment, together with the absolute \\((1,3,3,3)\\) form estimate, make expected tail cost \\(C/A\\) per normalized cell. Even the crude \\(O((K+1)^2)\\) position-depth sum is bounded.</p><p>Return to continuous burst functions\n\\[\ng_j(y)=\\sum_nG_j(n,(1-j)u+jv)1_{\\{|y-\\delta n|&lt;c\\delta\\}}.\n\\]\nFor \\(c&lt;1/20\\), a progression through four bursts has exact integer progression centers. Its offset polygon has fixed positive area. Sampling the kernel again incurs the same geometric error. Fast evolution changes no slow index, so the relevant-window support argument remains valid.</p><p>Apply the definition of \\(C_N(q)\\) to these burst functions with the original admissible kernels. Choose\n\\[\np_a=p_b=p_*=2+2(q-2),\\qquad\np_j=\\frac{2p_*}{p_*-2}\\quad(j\\ne a,b),\n\\]\nwhose reciprocals sum to one. Use fast exponent \\(p_*\\) for the heavy slot and \\(4\\) for regular slots. Fast progression Hölder, random Hölder of exponent four, spatial Hölder, and Lemma 8.1 bound the maximal integral.</p><p>Regular-slot norms are at most \\(C|E_j|^{1/p_j}\\). Clipping and unitarity give heavy-slot norm at most\n\\(C_qA^{1-2/p_*}|E_a|^{1/p_*}\\).\nSumming \\(J\\le C(K+1)\\) positions costs\n\\[\nA^{1-2/p_*}J^{1-2/p_*}\n\\le(K+2)^{11(q-2)/(q-1)}.\n\\]\nSum logarithmically many length levels and the previously bounded errors. All norm approximations pass by the fourth-moment and mixed-norm bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, Mixed-norm maximal estimate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\((X,\\mu),(W,\\nu)\\) be finite measure spaces, \\(1&lt;r,s,t&lt;\\infty\\), and \\(v\\in A_r(\\mathbb R)\\). Maximal operators act only in \\(y\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|M_yg\\|_{L_y^r(v;L_\\xi^s(X;L_w^t(W)))}\n\\le C_{r,s,t}([v]_{A_r})\n\\|g\\|_{L_y^r(v;L_\\xi^s(X;L_w^t(W)))}.\n\\]\nIn particular, for finite \\(p,b,h&gt;q\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\|M_qf\\|_{L_y^pL_\\xi^bL_w^h}\n\\le C_{p,b,h,q}\\|f\\|_{L_y^pL_\\xi^bL_w^h}.\n\\]\nConstants do not depend on numbers of atoms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the scalar weighted maximal theorem and the family-of-pairs Rubio de Francia extrapolation theorem cited in reference [1]. For bounded compactly supported \\(g\\), integrate the scalar weighted \\(L^t\\) bound in \\(w\\), obtaining pairs\n\\[\nU_\\xi(y)=\\|M_yg(y,\\xi,\\cdot)\\|_{L_w^t},\n\\quad\nV_\\xi(y)=\\|g(y,\\xi,\\cdot)\\|_{L_w^t}.\n\\]\nExtrapolate to exponent \\(s\\), integrate in \\(\\xi\\), and extrapolate the resulting pairs to exponent \\(r\\). This preserves the displayed norm order and requires no ordering of \\(r,s,t\\). Truncate and use monotone convergence for general \\(g\\). Apply to \\(|f|^q\\) with exponents \\(p/q,b/q,h/q\\), then take the \\(q\\)-th root.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 7.5, Side estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use one normalized attempt, active pattern, and localization leaf. Main budgets obey \\(\\log D_m\\le k^{O(1)}\\), histories have length \\(m=O(\\log L)\\), and\n  \\(G_0=\\exp(\\log^{O(1)}(2k))\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Partition depths into blocks \\([b,b+K)\\), \\(K\\le\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\), and choose \\(u_b\\le b\\), \\(b-u_b\\le CK\\). Outputs before the attempt are zero.</p><p class=\"li\">In a basic-\\(B\\) slot define rolling sides\n  \\[\n  S^{\\rm roll}_{l,G,s}=Z_{l,G,s}-Z_{l,G,u_b}.\n  \\]\n  A blockwise fixed linear side \\(V_{l,G,b}\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{G,b}\\|V_{l,G,b}\\|_2^2\\le G_0|R|.\n  \\]\n  This hypothesis is required anew on every normalized restart root, not inferred by restriction of a global family.</p><p class=\"li\">A side is a rolling side plus a linear side. In a basic-\\(U\\) slot the entire current output is a mandatory side.</p><p class=\"li\">Helper closure is performed after these whole side states are fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Fix \\(p\\in(2/3,1)\\). After common first-crossing cuts of arbitrarily small fixed total relative root length:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. terms with at least three side factors have length-weighted \\(p\\)-power cost at most \\(P^C|R|\\), if the fourth retained size is at most \\(P^C\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. exactly-two-side terms with whole current coarse complements have freeze, large-complement, and restoration errors of \\(p\\)-power cost at most \\(P^C|R|\\), transferred main cost at most \\(\\eta P^C|R|\\), and negligible numerical errors;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. the remaining coupled near-band sums have absolute cost at most\n   \\[\n   (1+C_N(q))C_qP^{-1/2}G_0(K+2)^{C(q-2)}|R|.\n   \\]\nAll estimates restart uniformly on normalized subroots. Subsequent cuts remove original summands without changing fixed stack functions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Three-side costs follow from Lemma 9.2. For exactly two sides, Proposition 7 gives transferred main terms, numerical errors, and near-band defects. Restore the full \\(Y\\)-pair in the main term; resulting errors have a third basic side, while the full pair gives \\(\\eta\\) against arbitrary side inputs. Side square caps give main cost \\(\\eta P^C|R|\\).</p><p>Freeze complements as in Lemma 9.2. For each fixed stack configuration, randomize the banded side positions by \\(\\epsilon_i\\zeta_p\\), untouched positions by \\(\\eta_j\\), and the frozen complementary band stack by \\(\\zeta_p\\). Multiply each kernel by selected position signs. Expectation selects precisely the required positions and paired bands. The coefficient is \\(O(1/P)\\), and iterated Khintchine gives Proposition 8’s moment bounds.</p><p>On each retained hull make a sparse stopping decomposition for the two fixed side stacks, stopping when either squared average exceeds sixteen times its root average. The stopped length is at most one eighth. Complete pieces by boundary children; every leaf has controlled side sizes, and frozen complementary sizes remain bounded. Each piece uses at most \\(K\\) depths. Apply Proposition 8.</p><p>If \\(a_Q,b_Q\\) are stack sizes at piece root \\(Q\\), sparse remainders \\(E_Q\\) are disjoint for a fixed hull and have size at least \\(7|Q|/8\\). Lemma 9.4 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\nu,Q}|Q|a_Qb_Q\n\\lesssim\\int_R\\sum_\\nu\\sqrt{m_{a,\\nu}m_{b,\\nu}}\n\\le P^{1/2}G_0|R|.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(P^{-1}\\), absorb logarithms and configurations in \\(G_0\\), and obtain the stated near-band estimate. Arbitrary initial cell signs make it an absolute bound for cell contributions while retaining paired band sums.</p><p>For the original cell minimum, numerical and near-band terms are estimated absolutely. Other terms use\n\\(\\min(\\eta,x)\\le\\eta^{1-p}x^p\\), after group sums are formed. All stopping budgets are nonnegative partial ancestor quantities, and the stated hypotheses are reconstructed on fresh normalized roots.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.3, Variation on certified endpoint cells", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(Z_{G,s}\\) be a basic coarse localized output. Consider disjoint or boundedly overlapping common depth intervals \\([r,t]\\). At each right endpoint restrict to a union \\(E_{G,r,t}\\) of \\(t\\)-cells with a downstream actual-use certificate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_{G,[r,t]}\n\\|1_{E_{G,r,t}}(Z_{G,t}-Z_{G,r})\\|_2^2\n\\le G_0\\|1_RF\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nFor a shared coarse history without outer ball, omit the group sum. Bounded overlap multiplies the constant accordingly.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand complements and couple pad choices across time. Jensen reduces to\n\\(Z_{G,s}=L_{G,s}B'_sF\\), with nested ball projections and a shared product of \\(O(m)\\) nested families. Split\n\\[\nZ_{G,t}-Z_{G,r}\n=(L_{G,t}-L_{G,r})B'_rF+L_{G,t}(B'_t-B'_r)F.\n\\]\nFor one group, ball increments on disjoint depth intervals are orthogonal rows. Their adjoints lie in sums of whole usable endpoint ranges; a right-endpoint certificate certifies earlier nonempty endpoints. Lemma 6.4 gives joint group Bessel, and Proposition 1 inserts \\(B'_r\\).</p><p>For the second term, first use usable-range Bessel over groups at the common right endpoint. Telescope the shared product; discard left contractions, leaving orthogonal middle increments with right factors evaluated at \\(r\\). Proposition 1 inserts those right factors. Sum \\(O(m)\\) terms, restore averages by Jensen, and use interval coloring for bounded overlap. The zero convention treats intervals crossing attempt start.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Unnumbered arguments of Sections 7.3–7.4:", "statement": "<p>Unnumbered arguments of Sections 7.3–7.4: side square budgets, power costs, and frozen complements.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 9’s side hypothesis and the basic-\\(U\\) square bound.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Integrated side-square costs are at most \\(P^C|R|\\). Outside small first-crossing root families, pathwise sums of squared local side sizes are at most \\(P^C\\). Three strong size factors therefore have total length-weighted \\(p\\)-power cost at most \\(P^C|R|\\).</p><p>In near-band defects, complementary band stacks and the other complement may be frozen at block start. Freeze errors and uses with large frozen complements, including large ancestor values in the block, have the same power-cost bound. On remaining uses, frozen complements have bounded local \\(L^2\\) size on their entire block hulls.</p>", "proof": "<p>Basic-\\(U\\) sides use current group Bessel and the basic square estimate. Rolling sides use Lemma 9.1 on \\([u_b,s]\\), with overlap \\(O(K)\\). Linear sides are counted at most \\(K\\) times per block. Integrate and stop at first partial path-square crossings.</p><p>If \\(a_s,b_s,c_s\\) are group-aggregated strong sizes, Cauchy–Schwarz in groups bounds a three-side form by \\(P^Ca_sb_sc_s\\). For \\(3p&gt;2\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_s(a_sb_sc_s)^p\n\\le\n\\Bigl(\\sum_sa_s^{3p}\\Bigr)^{1/3}\n\\Bigl(\\sum_sb_s^{3p}\\Bigr)^{1/3}\n\\Bigl(\\sum_sc_s^{3p}\\Bigr)^{1/3}\n\\le\\prod_{\\bullet=a,b,c}\\Bigl(\\sum_s\\bullet_s^2\\Bigr)^{p/2}.\n\\]\nUse the path caps and integrate. The power is taken after the group sum.</p><p>Current band projections are orthogonal, so current stack sizes are bounded. Lemma 9.1 bounds differences from block-start values, paying polynomially many bands and \\(O(K)\\) overlap. Split terminal ball complements into positive ball and shared identity terms; do not count shared histories once per group.</p><p>Every freeze error supplies a third strong factor. If another frozen complement is unbounded, write it as bounded current value plus its strong difference. If a frozen size exceeds twice the current bound, the square-root event indicator is bounded by a constant times the difference size. Certify all potential ancestor cells; propagating a flag through a block costs at most \\(K\\). Include these nonnegative power budgets in first-crossing caps.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.4, Side stacks", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a near offset \\(q-p\\), transfer step, and search path in Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The two varying sides are sums of small-overhead many configurations of fixed vector stacks for each \\((G,b)\\), with one position selected from each stack at a use. The banded side includes the band index. After absorbing configuration count in \\(G_0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{G,b}\\|\\operatorname{stack}_{a,G,b}\\|_2^2\\le PG_0|R|,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{G,b}\\|\\operatorname{stack}_{{\\rm untouched},G,b}\\|_2^2\\le G_0|R|.\n\\]\nStacks are fixed before later summation cuts.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(b\\le s&lt;b+K\\), write\n\\[\nW_s=W_b+\\sum_{[r,t]\\in\\mathcal P(b,s)}(W_t-W_r)\n\\]\nusing binary prefix intervals, at most one per length. Restrict fixed functions to the union of uses selecting them; this preserves their required values and decreases norms.</p><p>For basic-\\(U\\) inputs, use depths directly as positions and their square bound. Pay at most \\(P+1\\) indices on the banded side.</p><p>For a fixed linear side \\(V_{G,b}\\), subsequent products \\(T_s\\) satisfy, at a fixed binary length,\n\\[\n\\|T_bV\\|_2^2+\\sum_{[r,t]}\\|(T_t-T_r)V\\|_2^2\n\\le(C\\log(K+2))^{O(m)}\\|V\\|_2^2\n\\]\nby the nested-prefix product argument. Restore averages by Jensen and use the linear-side hypothesis.</p><p>For rolling inputs,\n\\[\nT_t(Z_t-Z_{u_b})-T_r(Z_r-Z_{u_b})\n=T_t(Z_t-Z_r)+(T_t-T_r)(Z_r-Z_{u_b}).\n\\]\nThe first term uses Lemma 9.1 on full certified endpoint cells. Expand the inner prefix \\([u_b,r]\\) for the second. At fixed outer and inner lengths, use each fixed inner increment as the input to the outer product-jump estimate. Sum those inputs by Lemma 9.1; block windows have bounded overlap. Length choices cost only small overhead. Individual pad choices remain averaged rather than becoming configurations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Unnumbered argument of Section 7.6, Hull maxima and common cuts.", "statement": "<p>Unnumbered argument of Section 7.6, Hull maxima and common cuts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For each stack index \\(\\nu=(G,b,\\text{configuration})\\), let its use hull consist of cells between maximal use roots and uses. Keep fixed stack functions unchanged after cuts. Suppose total energy is at most \\(E|R|\\) and total hull-root support length at most \\(M_0|R|\\), with \\(\\log M_0\\le k^{O(1)}\\). Define\n\\[\nm_\\nu(x)=\\sup_{\\substack{I\\text{ in its hull}\\\\x\\in I}}\n\\fint_I\\|\\operatorname{stack}_\\nu\\|^2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Small common first-crossing cuts yield joint retained hull caps of order\n\\(E(1+\\log(2+M_0))\\). For the two stack families these are \\(PG_0\\) and \\(G_0\\), everywhere on full retained hull intervals, including portions below cuts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Weak \\(L^1\\) for the dyadic maximal operator gives\n\\[\n\\sum_\\nu|\\{m_\\nu&gt;T\\}|\n\\le\\min(M_0|R|,CE|R|/T).\n\\]\nFirst cut individual crossings of \\(C_1E\\), costing \\(C/C_1\\) of root length. Integrating the distribution bound up to \\(C_1E\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_\\nu\\int_R\\min(m_\\nu,C_1E)\n\\le CE(1+\\log(2+C_1M_0))|R|.\n\\]\nMarkov gives a joint cap at a further small cost.</p><p>Implement stops on partial ancestor suprema in increasing depth, removing the triggering cell before its value enters a retained cap. Consequently earlier retained ancestors remain controlled even inside a stopped region. Combine these cuts with partial power-budget crossings. Large-frozen-complement flags are charged inside powered estimates and do not by themselves create restart roots.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 8.9, Square energy of the linear side", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one localization leaf on a normalized root. Basic-\\(B\\) outputs are\n  \\[\n  Z_{l,G,s}=\\overline L_{l,G,s}\\mathcal B_{l,s}B_{l,s},\n  \\]\n  with main logarithmic budget \\(k^{O(1)}\\). Main endpoint ranges have downstream certificates and satisfy Lemma 6.4.</p><p class=\"li\">Partition depths into dyadic-length blocks \\([b,b+K)\\), and put\n  \\[\n  u_b=b-4K,\\qquad v_b=b-2K,\\qquad t_b=b+K.\n  \\]\n  Freeze output coefficients at \\(u_b\\), using zero before attempt start; retain \\(v_b\\)-cells only if a descendant actual use occurs in the block.</p><p class=\"li\">A primary is an individual main column available to the maximal outer ball pad. At its first match choose one expansion permanently and inherit all mode labels.</p><p class=\"li\">For group primary center \\(z\\), choose \\(Q_0\\) clearing \\(C_z\\), put \\(\\vartheta=\\theta_z/Q_0\\), and, for offset \\(\\rho\\),\n  \\[\n  d_\\rho(y)=\\rho+\\{\\theta_zy-\\rho\\},\\quad\n  p^\\rho(y)=\\lambda_zy^2+(\\theta_zy)^TC_zd_\\rho(y).\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\zeta^\\rho\\) vanish near branch cuts at scale \\(\\delta_c=\\exp(-k^{C_c})\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb E_\\rho\\zeta^\\rho=c_\\zeta\\ge1/2,\\qquad\n  \\mathbb E_\\rho|1-(\\zeta^\\rho)^2|^2\\le Cd_{\\max}\\delta_c.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Lemma 10.1 supplies a bounded block-independent lift \\(\\mathcal R_{l,G}\\) and augmented modes. Set\n  \\(\\widetilde Z_{l,G,b}=\\mathcal R_{l,G}Z_{l,G,u_b}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(D_*\\ge e^k\\), \\(\\log D_*\\le\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\), to cover mode counts, coefficient sums, rational heights, inverse private weights, \\(Q_0\\), and Fourier coefficient sums. Truncate cutoff and progression-multiplier series to tail \\(D_*^{-300}\\), with cutoff \\(B_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\Delta=10\\), a sufficiently small absolute \\(\\delta_*&gt;0\\), and\n  \\[\n  C_*=(10d_{\\max}D_*)^{100d_{\\max}^4},\\quad\n  R_*^{\\rm ht}=\\lceil\\log_2C_*\\rceil+2\\Delta+10.\n  \\]\n  Choose \\(J_*\\) sufficiently large compared with\n  \\(\\delta_*^{-2}d_{\\max}R_*^{\\rm ht}\\log(2D_m)\\).\n  Put \\(L_h=6^h\\), \\(E_h=A_0^{h+1}\\), with \\(A_0\\) a sufficiently large fixed power of \\(C_*6^{R_*^{\\rm ht}}B_*D_*\\), and \\(\\Xi=D_*^{1000}\\).\n  Choose rational height reserve\n  \\[\n  H&gt;(d_{\\max}C_*E_{J_*}B_*L_{J_*}Q_0\\Xi)^{10},\n  \\]\n  gap \\(T&gt;(d_{\\max}H)^{Cd_{\\max}^4}\\Xi\\), then dyadic \\(K\\le\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\) larger than the height-packet width plus \\(2\\log_2T\\), with sufficient inverse-\\(D_*\\) lag reserve.</p><p class=\"li\">For mode \\(m\\), let \\(\\omega_m(x)=2\\lambda_mx+\\gamma_m\\). At height \\(h\\), on a retained \\(v_b\\)-cell \\(J\\), the PRE and CURRENT graphs join modes when, for integral \\(|n|_\\infty\\le E_h\\),\n  \\[\n  \\sup_{x\\in J}|\\omega_m(x)-\\omega_{m'}(x)+n\\cdot\\vartheta/L_h|\n  \\le\\Xi/r_{v_b}\n  \\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n  \\le\\Xi/r_{t_b},\n  \\]\n  respectively. Write \\(\\mathcal E_h(m),\\mathcal F_h(m)\\) for components and \\(\\pi\\) for their primary-label sets.</p><p class=\"li\">A mode is good at \\(h\\ge\\Delta\\) when the floor logarithms, to base \\(1.1\\), of\n  \\(|\\pi(\\mathcal E_{h-\\Delta}(m))|\\) and \\(|\\pi(\\mathcal F_h(m))|\\) agree, and at least nine tenths of the latter primaries are nonexceptional and height-good as defined below. Heights below \\(\\Delta\\) are bad.</p><p class=\"li\">Core modes have at most \\(\\delta_*(J_*+1)\\) bad heights; all other modes are linear-side modes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Let \\(V_{l,G,b}\\) be the exact subtotal of the linear-side modes in \\(\\widetilde Z_{l,G,b}\\), including private coordinates, on retained \\(v_b\\)-cells, and zero on branches without a needed output or certificate. Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{G,b}\\|V_{l,G,b}\\|_2^2\\le G_0|R|,\n\\qquad G_0=\\exp(\\log^{O(1)}(2k)).\n\\]\nThe bound is uniform in offsets and holds anew on normalized restart roots. It supplies Proposition 9’s linear-side hypothesis.</p>", "proof": "<p>An event is \\((b,J,\\mathcal E)\\), where \\(J\\) is a retained pre-cell and \\(\\mathcal E\\) a minimal PRE component of side modes. Lemma 10.4 makes each such component entirely side.</p><p>Split block indices into ten congruence classes. Link an event to a later descendant event if some modes agree or are adjacent in the later minimal PRE graph. Earlier CURRENT edges persist into later PRE, since the later pre-depth exceeds the earlier comparison depth. Along a linked chain,\n\\[\n\\pi(\\mathcal E_h^{(i)})\n\\subseteq\\pi(\\mathcal F_h^{(i)})\n\\subseteq\\pi(\\mathcal E_h^{(i+1)})\n\\]\nat every height.</p><p>Apply Lemma 10.6 with\n\\[\nM=D_m,\\quad\\beta=1/10,\\quad\nA=Cd_{\\max}R_*^{\\rm ht},\\quad B=Cd_{\\max},\\quad\\delta=\\delta_*.\n\\]\nLemmas 10.2–10.3 give the multiplicities, and the choice of \\(J_*\\) absorbs the height factor. Chain length is at most \\(C_{\\delta_*}d_{\\max}\\log(2D_m)\\), a small overhead.</p><p>Give events longest-chain levels. At a fixed level, overlapping events have no shared or adjacent modes, with path label count at most \\(D_*\\). Lemma 10.7 gives Bessel event rows. Lemma 10.8 inserts the frozen main histories:\n\\[\n\\sum_{G,b,J,\\mathcal E}\n\\|P_{\\mathcal E,J}\\widetilde Z_{l,G,b}\\|_2^2\n\\le G_0|R|.\n\\]\nAn exact coefficient subtotal \\(T_{\\mathcal E,J}\\) differs from its projection by at most \\(D_*^{-190}\\) in normalized \\(L^2(J)\\): all outside modes are PRE-nonadjacent, and private conditioning and total coefficient sum absorb the pair bound. Path occurrence counts make summed additive errors negligible. Minimal PRE subtotals on a cell are almost orthogonal by the same calculation. Their sum is \\(V_{l,G,b}\\), proving the asserted energy bound uniformly in offsets.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1, First-match expansion and bounded lift", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Statement.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At a primary \\(\\Phi\\)’s first match \\(I_0\\),\n\\[\n\\zeta^\\rho\\phi_\\Phi e(-c_lp^\\rho)\n=\\sum_ma_{\\Phi,m}e(\\lambda_my^2+\\gamma_my)+O(\\delta_c),\n\\]\nwith one common \\(\\lambda_m\\) for that primary. Use augmented modes\n\\[\n\\psi_m=\n\\bigl(\\zeta^\\rho e(c_lp^\\rho)e(\\lambda_my^2+\\gamma_my),\n\\sqrt{\\epsilon_m}e_m\\bigr).\n\\]\nA budget \\(D_1\\), \\(\\log D_1\\le\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\), bounds mode number, coefficient sum, \\(|\\lambda_m||I_0|^2\\), and rational height in\n\\[\n\\gamma_m-\\gamma_{m'}=a\\cdot(\\theta_v,\\vartheta).\n\\]\nThere is a uniformly bounded pointwise, block-independent linear lift mapping frozen main outputs exactly to their mode synthesis.</p>", "proof": "<p>The branch cutoff makes the conjugating factor a smooth bounded-branch chart. The short-path endpoint conclusion of Lemma 6.1 supplies a splitting at \\(I_0\\). Choose it once and apply Lemma 3.4 with tail \\(\\delta_c\\). A single splitting gives one quadratic coefficient. Recentring adds a common unrestricted linear shift, so mode differences have the displayed rational dependence.</p><p>Write \\(\\Phi=(\\phi_\\Phi,\\sqrt{\\epsilon_\\Phi}e_\\Phi)\\) and\n\\[\nq_\\Phi=\\sum_ma_{\\Phi,m}(\\psi_m)_{\\rm phys}\n-(\\zeta^\\rho)^2\\phi_\\Phi,\\qquad |q_\\Phi|\\le\\delta_c.\n\\]\nDefine the lift’s physical coordinate by\n\\[\n(\\zeta^\\rho)^2F_{\\rm phys}\n+\\sum_{\\Phi\\ {\\rm matched}}\n\\frac{F_\\Phi}{\\sqrt{\\epsilon_\\Phi}}q_\\Phi,\n\\]\nand mode coordinate \\(m\\) by\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\epsilon_m}a_{\\Phi,m}\n\\frac{F_\\Phi}{\\sqrt{\\epsilon_\\Phi}}.\n\\]\nMain count and conditioning make the physical correction uniformly bounded. Choose \\(\\epsilon_m\\) small enough that the mode-coordinate map has norm at most one; its logarithmic inverse remains \\(\\operatorname{poly}(P,k)\\).</p><p>For an outer output \\(\\sum_\\Phi\\beta_\\Phi\\Phi\\), its private coordinate is exactly \\(\\sqrt{\\epsilon_\\Phi}\\beta_\\Phi\\), so substitution yields\n\\(\\sum_{\\Phi,m}\\beta_\\Phi a_{\\Phi,m}\\psi_m\\). The lift is pointwise and is not commuted through projections.</p><p>For later core use, four matching gauge factors have a smooth bounded periodic progression multiplier. Indeed, carries\n\\(k_j=d_j-(1-j)d_0-jd_1\\) are bounded integers, and quadratic cancellation leaves\n\\[\n\\prod_je(c_jp^{\\rho_j}(y_j))\n=e\\!\\left(\\sum_jc_j(\\theta_zy_j)^TC_zk_j\\right).\n\\]\nDenominator clearing makes each carry piece a torus character. Enlarge cutoffs and extend by zero at cuts; this works at every anchor. Its coefficient sum is budgeted before truncation, as required in Proposition 10.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2, Exceptional blocks and tiny rational space", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For primary horizontal vector \\(\\Theta=(\\theta_v,\\vartheta)\\), a block is nonexceptional if every height-\\(H\\) rational vector \\(a\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|a\\cdot\\Theta|\\le T^{-1}/r_{v_b}\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n|a\\cdot\\Theta|\\ge T/r_{t_b}.\n\\]\nLet \\(K_{\\rm rat}\\) be the rational span of height-\\(H\\) tiny vectors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A primary is exceptional in at most \\(Cd_{\\max}\\) blocks on a path. At a nonexceptional block, every height-\\(H\\) vector in \\(K_{\\rm rat}\\) itself satisfies the tiny alternative.</p>", "proof": "<p>For nonzero evaluations let \\(x(a)=\\log_2(|a\\cdot\\Theta|r_0)\\). Exception at block start \\(b\\) means\n\\(b-2K-\\log_2T&lt;x(a)&lt;b+K+\\log_2T\\).\nProposition 2 covers all \\(x(a)\\) by at most \\(d_{\\max}\\) packets of width \\(W\\). Since \\(K\\ge W+2\\log_2T\\), a packet affects at most five \\(K\\)-spaced starts.</p><p>Choose a basis of tiny vectors. Cramer’s rule expresses another height-\\(H\\) vector in their span with total coefficient magnitude at most\n\\((d_{\\max}H)^{Cd_{\\max}^3}\\).\nIts evaluation is therefore below the huge threshold by the gap choice. The dichotomy forces it to be tiny.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, Height stabilization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>At a nonexceptional block put\n\\[\nK_B=\\{w:(0,w)\\in K_{\\rm rat}\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Except for at most \\(Cd_{\\max}R_*^{\\rm ht}\\) heights, two modes of the primary in one CURRENT component at height \\(h\\) are directly adjacent in PRE at \\(h-\\Delta\\). Such heights are height-good for the primary.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(R'=R_*^{\\rm ht}\\) and discard \\(h&lt;R'\\). Sum CURRENT witnesses along a simple path of at most \\(D_*\\) edges. Since endpoint modes of one primary have equal quadratic part,\n\\[\n|a\\cdot\\Theta+m_h\\cdot\\vartheta/L_h|\n\\le D_*\\Xi/r_{t_b},\\qquad |m_h|_\\infty\\le D_*E_h.\n\\]\nThe rational vector has height at most \\(H\\), so the gap gives\n\\(a+(0,m_h/L_h)\\in K_{\\rm rat}\\).</p><p>Let \\(V_i\\) be the span of height-\\(D_*\\) vectors \\(q'\\) admitting\n\\(q'+(0,m/L_i)\\in K_{\\rm rat}\\), \\(|m|_\\infty\\le D_*E_i\\).\nLet \\(U_i\\) be the span of integer \\(K_B\\)-vectors of size at most \\(C_*^3D_*E_i\\).\nBoth increase. Discard heights where either rank changes in \\([h-R',h]\\), at most \\(2d_{\\max}R'\\).</p><p>Express \\(a=\\sum_jb_ja_j\\) using defining vectors at \\(h-R'\\), with coefficients and common denominator at most \\(C_*\\). Subtracting their relations gives\n\\[\nm_h-6^{R'}\\sum_jb_jm_j\\in K_B.\n\\]\nAfter clearing denominators this belongs to \\(U_h=U_{h-R'}=:U\\).\nThe lattice \\(U\\cap\\mathbb Z^{\\dim\\theta_z}\\) is saturated, so its quotient is free. In quotient coordinates,\n\\([m_h]=6^{R'}\\sum_jb_j[m_j]\\), whose integral entries are divisible by \\(6^\\Delta\\) by the denominator bound. Lift \\([m_h]/6^\\Delta\\) to an integer \\(m'\\).</p><p>Round along a small integer spanning set of \\(U\\), obtaining\n\\[\n|m'|_\\infty\n\\le6^{R'-\\Delta}\\sum_j|b_j||m_j|_\\infty\n+d_{\\max}C_*^3D_*E_{h-R'}\n\\le E_{h-\\Delta}.\n\\]\nThen \\(m_h-6^\\Delta m'\\in K_B\\), so\n\\(a+(0,m'/L_{h-\\Delta})\\in K_{\\rm rat}\\).\nIts height is at most \\(H\\); Lemma 10.2 makes its evaluation tiny, hence below PRE threshold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.4, Coherence of good predicates", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Statement.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Good modes in one CURRENT component at height \\(h\\) have the same PRE component at \\(h-\\Delta\\). Core/side status is constant on minimal PRE components. Core modes in one minimal CURRENT component have identical good predicates and the same PRE components at \\(h-\\Delta\\) throughout\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{\\rm up}\n=\\{h:\\lceil4\\delta_*J_*\\rceil\\le h\\le J_*\\}.\n\\]\nEvery core mode has bad density at most \\(2\\delta_*\\) in this upper range.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(F=\\pi(\\mathcal F_h)\\), a good predecessor has more than \\(|F|/1.1\\) primaries. Two such predecessor sets intersect in more than \\((2/1.1-1)|F|\\), hence contain a common nonexceptional height-good primary. Its witnessing modes join by Lemma 10.3, identifying predecessors.</p><p>Minimal PRE components have identical larger components and predicates. For two core modes in a minimal CURRENT component, their total bad heights are at most \\(2\\delta_*(J_*+1)\\); below \\(4\\delta_*J_*\\) they have a common good height. The preceding argument identifies their PRE components there, and nesting identifies all later components and predicates. The upper-range size gives the density bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.5", "orig": "Lemma 8.5, Entry-rank summation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let nonempty finite sets \\(S_1\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq S_T\\) have final size at most \\(M\\). Let \\(X_t\\subseteq S_t\\), with each element in at most \\(A\\) of the \\(X_t\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_{t=1}^T\\frac{|X_t|}{|S_t|}\n\\le AH_M,\\qquad H_M=\\sum_{r=1}^{\\lfloor M\\rfloor}\\frac1r\\le1+\\log M.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Enumerate elements by first entry, breaking ties arbitrarily. A containing set for entry rank \\(r\\) has at least \\(r\\) elements. That element contributes at most \\(A/r\\). Sum over ranks.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.6", "orig": "Lemma 8.6, Bad-density chains", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For positions \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) and heights \\(0\\le h\\le J\\), let nonempty primary sets \\(P_{i,h},C_{i,h}\\), increasing in \\(h\\), have size at most \\(M\\), and\n\\[\nP_{i,h}\\subseteq C_{i,h}\\subseteq P_{i+1,h}.\n\\]\nEach primary is exceptional at at most \\(B\\) positions. At a fixed position, each nonexceptional primary is height-bad at at most \\(A\\) heights. Declare \\(h&lt;\\Delta\\) bad, and other heights bad when the predecessor/current floor logs differ or the exceptional-or-height-bad fraction exceeds \\(\\beta\\).\nEvery position has more than \\(\\delta(J+1)\\) bad heights. Put\n\\[\nL_0=\\lfloor\\log_{1.1}M\\rfloor,\\qquad\nQ_0=\\Delta(L_0+1)+\\beta^{-1}AH_M.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(J+1\\ge2Q_0/\\delta\\), then\n\\[\nn&lt;\\frac2\\delta(L_0+\\beta^{-1}BH_M).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(p_{i,h},c_{i,h}\\) for floor logs. The first bad indicator is at most\n\\[\nc_{i,h}-p_{i,h-\\Delta}\n=(p_{i,h}-p_{i,h-\\Delta})+(c_{i,h}-p_{i,h}).\n\\]\nThe first term sums to at most \\(n\\Delta L_0\\); temporal nesting makes the second sum at each height at most \\(L_0\\). Including initial bad heights gives \\(n\\Delta(L_0+1)+(J+1)L_0\\).</p><p>Bound the fraction failure by \\(\\beta^{-1}\\) times exceptional and height-bad fractions. Lemma 10.5 bounds height-bad fractions by \\(AH_M\\) at each position and exceptional fractions by \\(BH_M\\) at each height. Thus bad incidences are at most\n\\(nQ_0+(J+1)(L_0+\\beta^{-1}BH_M)\\).\nCompare with \\(\\delta n(J+1)\\) and absorb \\(nQ_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.7", "orig": "Lemma 8.7, Separated mode rows", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a role and group. On dyadic cells \\(J_e\\), choose nonempty available mode sets \\(M_e\\), pairwise disjoint along paths, with total path cardinality at most \\(D_*\\). Cross pairs on distinct overlapping cells, or in distinct sets on the same cell, have normalized inner products on the smaller cell at most \\(D_*^{-200}\\). Let \\(P_e\\) project onto their restrictions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sum_e\\|P_eF\\|_2^2\\le C\\|F\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThe cross-pair hypothesis follows from no shared or adjacent modes in the later PRE graph on associated pre-cells, or in the later CURRENT graph on actual-use cells.</p>", "proof": "<p>The common gauge cancels in a pair of physical modes. Fourier-expand \\((\\zeta^\\rho)^2\\), with coefficient sum at most \\(D_*\\), shifts allowed by the graph reserve, and tail \\(D_*^{-300}\\). Nonadjacency gives phase derivative above the graph threshold somewhere; slope variation\n\\[\nr_{v_b}\\operatorname{osc}_J\\omega_m\\le CD_*2^{-4K}\n\\]\nmakes it at least half the threshold everywhere. First-derivative integration gives \\(C/\\Xi\\), including on actual cells since \\(|I|\\ge r_{t_b}\\). Thus the pair bound is at most\n\\(CD_*/\\Xi+D_*^{-300}\\le D_*^{-200}\\). Private coordinates are orthogonal.</p><p>By duality synthesize \\(f_e=1_{J_e}\\sum_{m\\in M_e}\\beta_{e,m}\\psi_m\\). Put \\(b_e=(\\sum_m|\\beta_{e,m}|^2)^{1/2}\\). Private coordinates give\n\\(|J_e|b_e^2\\le D_*^2\\|f_e\\|_2^2\\).\nCross terms are at most \\(D_*^{-199}|J_e\\cap J_f|b_eb_f\\). At most \\(D_*\\) rows occur on a path, so their total is at most\n\\(D_*^{-196}\\sum_e\\|f_e\\|_2^2\\).\nAdd the diagonal and dualize.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.8", "orig": "Lemma 8.8, Composition with the lift and main histories", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For each group, mode projection rows \\(P_e\\), supported on \\(J_e\\), have squared Bessel bound \\(G_0\\). Let \\(J_e\\subseteq u_e\\), where \\(u_e\\) is a whole usable main endpoint cell with a downstream certificate; row modes are available at \\(u_e\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The frozen outputs\n\\[\nP_e\\mathcal R_{l,G}\\overline L_{l,G,u_e}\n\\mathcal B_{l,u_e}B_{l,u_e}\n\\]\nhave joint squared sum at most \\(G_0|R|\\), over groups and rows.</p>", "proof": "<p>For one group, \\(P_e\\mathcal R_{l,G}\\) is Bessel because the lift is fixed and bounded. It is local to \\(u_e\\). Proposition 1 inserts the outer individual main ball projection, with only a power of \\(\\log(2D_m)\\); restore its average in the row Hilbert sum.</p><p>The resulting group row adjoints have ranges in whole usable main ball spaces, in the order\n\\(L_{l,G,u_e}\\mathcal R_{l,G}^*P_e\\).\nLemma 6.4 makes them jointly Bessel across groups. Apply Proposition 1 again, with decision cells \\(u_e\\), to insert shared histories and basic constituent projections. Their lengths are \\(O(\\log L)\\), and all losses are \\(G_0\\). The original input has squared norm at most \\(|R|\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 8.12, Top count", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 10’s mode construction. For \\(h\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm up}\\), a CURRENT component is successful if it contains a good core mode. Its unique good predecessor is \\(\\mathcal E_{h-\\Delta}\\), and its type is\n\\[\n\\left(\n\\lfloor\\log_{1.1}|\\pi(\\mathcal E_{h-\\Delta})|\\rfloor,\n\\lfloor\\log_{1.1}|\\mathcal E_{h-\\Delta}|\\rfloor\n\\right).\n\\]\nOn an actual cell \\(I\\), for successful \\(\\mathcal F=\\mathcal F_h\\), define\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\mathcal F}^2\n=\\sum_{\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\mathcal F}\n\\left(\n\\|A_{\\mathcal D}\\|_{2,I}\n+D_*^{-30}\\sum_{\\substack{m\\ {\\rm core}\\\\m\\in\\mathcal D}}|\\alpha_m|\n\\right)^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal D\\) ranges over minimal CURRENT components and \\(A_{\\mathcal D}\\) is the full augmented core subtotal.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After small first-crossing cuts, fix a core role \\(j\\), height \\(h\\), type, and dyadic amplitude bin\n\\(\\sigma_j\\le\\sigma_{\\mathcal F}&lt;2\\sigma_j\\).\nIf \\(\\mathcal A_j^G\\) is the set of distinct inherited representative labels actually used in that bin on a retained path, then\n\\[\n\\sum_G|\\mathcal A_j^G|\n\\le\\min(D_*,P^C\\sigma_j^{-2}).\n\\]\nOn retained uses, all component and full side sizes are at most \\(P^C\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{s,G}\\|S_{l,G,s}\\|_{2,I_s}^2\\le P^C.\n\\]\nDistinct representatives of fixed role, group, height, and type satisfy, on comparable actual cells,\n\\[\n|\\omega_a(x_*)-\\omega_{a'}(x_*)+n\\cdot\\vartheta/L_h|\n&gt;\\frac{\\Xi}{2}\\max(|I|^{-1},|I'|^{-1}),\n\\quad |n|_\\infty\\le E_h.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a height, type, and threshold \\(D_*^{-1}\\le\\sigma\\le D_*^2\\). For every representative take the first actual-use cells on each branch where \\(\\sigma_{\\mathcal F}\\ge\\sigma\\), called first tops. Same-representative tops are disjoint; different-representative overlapping tops have cross-component CURRENT separation by Lemma 11.2. Lemma 10.7 gives Bessel rows \\(P_{\\mathcal F,I}\\), and Lemma 10.8, using the whole frozen decision cells certified by these tops, gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\rm tops}\\|P_{\\mathcal F,I}\\widetilde Z\\|_2^2\\le G_0|R|.\n\\]\nFor linear-side parts, sum disjoint actual cells at each depth, use Lemma 11.3, then sum at most \\(K\\) depths per block and Proposition 10:\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\rm tops}|I|\\|V_{\\mathcal F}\\|_{2,I}^2\\le KG_0|R|.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\sigma^2\\sum_{\\rm tops}|I|\n\\le C(1+K)G_0|R|+CD_*^{-50}\\sum_{\\rm tops}|I|.\n\\]\nAbsorb the last term since \\(\\sigma^2\\ge D_*^{-2}\\).</p><p>There are polynomially many height, type, and threshold choices in \\(P,k\\). Markov and first partial top-count crossings give, outside small root families, at most \\(P^C\\sigma^{-2}\\) tops on a path for each choice. A used representative in \\([\\sigma_j,2\\sigma_j)\\) has a retained first \\(\\sigma_j\\)-top above it. Hence representatives are bounded by top count, and also by full mode budget \\(D_*\\). Below the tested threshold range the latter bound suffices; above it bins are empty after fixed budget enlargement.</p><p>Lemma 11.1 supplies side square caps. The bounded lift, rolling bounds, and Lemma 11.3 give the retained amplitude upper bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Unnumbered argument of Section 8.7, Fixing offsets and separating side terms.", "statement": "<p>Unnumbered argument of Section 8.7, Fixing offsets and separating side terms.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Offsets can be fixed so that physical lift errors have negligible integrated square energy over all original actual uses, while Proposition 10 remains valid. With \\(A_{l,G,b}\\) the core subtotal, put\n\\[\nS_{l,G,s}=Z_{l,G,s}-Z_{l,G,u_b}+V_{l,G,b}\n\\]\nin basic-\\(B\\) roles; basic-\\(U\\) roles remain mandatory sides. Then, in physical coordinates,\n\\[\nZ_{l,G,s}=A_{l,G,b}+S_{l,G,s}+\\mathcal E_{l,G,b}.\n\\]\nAll physical-error terms are negligible. Terms with at most two core roles are covered by Proposition 9, after replacing two core complements by whole current arguments in exactly-two-side terms.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(Z_{l,G,u_b}=\\sum_\\Phi\\beta_\\Phi\\Phi\\), the explicit lift gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{l,G,b}\n=(1-(\\zeta^\\rho)^2)(Z_{l,G,u_b})_{\\rm phys}\n-\\sum_\\Phi\\beta_\\Phi q_\\Phi.\n\\]\nThe main coefficients, their suprema, and the original actual uses were fixed before offsets. On their own frozen cells coefficient sums and physical suprema are at most \\(D_m^C\\), without a lag loss. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\rho|\\mathcal E_{l,G,b}(y)|^2\n\\le D_m^Cd_{\\max}\\delta_c.\n\\]\nSum over original cell/group uses and enlarge \\(C_c\\), obtaining arbitrarily small inverse-main-budget energy. Choose offsets realizing this bound. Later selections only discard nonnegative terms; no independence from those selections is required. Proposition 10 is uniform in offsets.</p><p>Proposition 9’s rolling bounds and side pruning give path square caps. The bounded lift and \\(Z_{u_b}=Z_s-(Z_s-Z_{u_b})\\) bound core sizes. Lemma 3.1 and Cauchy–Schwarz make physical-error terms negligible.</p><p>Expand localized forms. Retain at least-three-core terms. With exactly two sides, replace core complements by whole current \\(Z\\); corrections have at least three sides. Proposition 9, via Proposition 7, estimates these and all other side terms. Restoring complementary basic inputs gives precisely the full pair from Proposition 5.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.10, Hereditary representatives", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a height, role, group, and type.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Choose a representative mode in each successful predecessor so that it is inherited whenever an earlier successful predecessor of that type on the path is contained in the current one. Distinct representatives on comparable actual cells have no adjacent cross pair of component modes in the later CURRENT graph, and satisfy the separation inequality in Proposition 11.</p>", "proof": "<p>If an earlier successful component links to a later one by equality or CURRENT adjacency, earlier CURRENT persistence gives \\(\\mathcal F_{\\rm early}\\subseteq\\mathcal F_{\\rm late}\\).\nTheir common primary type and later goodness imply predecessor primary sizes exceeding \\(|F|/1.1\\) and \\(|F|/1.1^2\\), where \\(F=\\pi(\\mathcal F_{\\rm late})\\). Their intersection exceeds one tenth of \\(|F|\\), so contains a later nonexceptional height-good primary. Lemma 10.3 joins its two witnesses in later PRE; thus\n\\(\\mathcal E_{\\rm early}\\subseteq\\mathcal E_{\\rm late}\\).</p><p>Process pre-cells in block order, inheriting when possible. Any two earlier predecessors contained in the current one each have more than half its mode count by the second type coordinate, so intersect and hence are nested by the preceding argument. Their inherited representatives agree, proving consistency.</p><p>A link between different representatives would force inheritance, so all cross modes are nonadjacent. At the later pre-cell, each allowed shifted derivative exceeds \\(\\Xi/r_{t_b}\\) somewhere. Negligible slope oscillation makes it exceed half that value at \\(x_*\\). Since the later actual cell has length at least \\(r_{t_b}\\), the displayed separation follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.11, Component-size comparison", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(P_{\\mathcal F,I}\\) project onto all augmented modes of a successful component, and \\(V_{\\mathcal F}\\) be its exact linear-side subtotal.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\sigma_{\\mathcal F}^2\n\\lesssim\n\\|P_{\\mathcal F,I}\\widetilde Z_{l,G,b}\\|_{2,I}^2\n+\\|V_{\\mathcal F}\\|_{2,I}^2+D_*^{-50},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathcal F}\\|V_{\\mathcal F}\\|_{2,I}^2\n\\lesssim\\|V_{l,G,b}\\|_{2,I}^2.\n\\]\nThe same almost orthogonality holds for arbitrary coefficient subtotals in distinct minimal CURRENT components.</p>", "proof": "<p>Distinct minimal CURRENT components are nonadjacent. Lemma 10.7’s oscillatory and private-coordinate calculation on \\(I\\) gives two-sided almost orthogonality of arbitrary subtotals in those components.</p><p>If \\(T_{\\mathcal F}\\) is the full coefficient subtotal, its core part is\n\\(T_{\\mathcal F}-V_{\\mathcal F}\\).\nThe coefficient floors contribute at most\n\\(CD_*^{-60}(\\sum_m|\\alpha_m|)^2\\le CD_*^{-58}\\).\nOutside modes are CURRENT-nonadjacent, so\n\\(\\|P_{\\mathcal F,I}\\widetilde Z-T_{\\mathcal F}\\|_{2,I}\\le D_*^{-190}\\).\nCombine these estimates and the squared triangle inequality. Decompose the entire linear side by CURRENT components and discard nonsuccessful ones to obtain the second bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 9.1, Sparse mass of separated progression tests", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>There are four roles \\(0,1,2,3\\), with either four or exactly three core roles \\(\\mathcal J\\). Let \\(M,L\\ge1\\), \\(d\\ge0\\), \\(B\\ge1\\), \\(0&lt;\\mu\\le1\\le\\nu\\), and \\(\\zeta\\in\\mathbb R^d\\). Each core role has a finite label set \\(A_i\\), injectively identified with real numbers, \\(|A_i|\\le M\\).</p><p>For finitely many distinct integers \\(s\\), let \\(R_s=R_02^s\\),\n\\(\\mathcal E_s\\subseteq\\prod_{i\\in\\mathcal J}A_i\\), and \\(N_s=|\\mathcal E_s|\\). Assume:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. two labels in distinct core roles determine at most one full core tuple over all scales, and the pair has at most \\(L\\) scale occurrences;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. if \\(l\\) is the side role, there are \\(w_s\\ge0\\), \\(\\sum_sw_s\\le M\\), such that every fixed core label is incident to at most \\(w_s\\) tuples at scale \\(s\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. distinct same-role labels occurring at scales \\(s,t\\) satisfy\n   \\[\n   |a-a'+n\\cdot\\zeta|&gt;\\Sigma\\max(R_s,R_t)\n   \\quad(|n|_\\infty\\le Q);\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. for each triple \\(\\mathcal T\\subseteq\\mathcal J\\), with excluded role \\(l'\\) and \\(\\alpha_i=i-l'\\), each tuple has a witness \\(|n|_\\infty\\le B\\) with\n   \\[\n   \\mu R_s\\le\\left|\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal T}\\alpha_ia_i+36n\\cdot\\zeta\\right|\n   \\le\\nu R_s;\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. at every occupied scale,\n   \\[\n   |n\\cdot\\zeta|\\notin[\\mu R_s/1000,1000\\nu R_s]\n   \\quad(|n|_\\infty\\le Q).\n   \\]\nSuppose \\(Q\\ge C_{\\rm res}(d+1)^2B\\) and\n\\(\\Sigma\\ge C_{\\rm res}(d+1)^2\\nu\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For\n\\(\\ell=1+\\log_2(2+(d+1)^2\\nu/\\mu)\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{s:N_s\\le\\delta M^2}N_s\n\\le C\\ell L^{29}\\delta^{1/2080}M^2,\n\\qquad0&lt;\\delta\\le1.\n\\]\nConstants and exponents are independent of dimension, frequencies, scales, and label cardinalities.</p>", "proof": "<p>First place finite real data on one sufficiently fine common grid. Approximate labels and frequencies on a fixed multiple of the step so divisions by \\(1,2,3\\) stay on the grid. Relax separation and annular constants by fixed factors and preserve the narrower pure-shift gap. The step is chosen after all finite data, and no bound depends on it.</p><p>Pair determination gives total mass at most \\(LM^2\\). Lemma 12.2 loses at most \\(4J_0\\kappa M^2\\). Lemma 12.3 controls nonfuture pairs and future combinations. Therefore\n\\[\nM^{-2}\\sum_{N_s\\le\\delta M^2}N_s\n\\le C\\left(J_0\\kappa+\\sqrt{L\\alpha}\n+\\ell LJ_0^4K_1^4\\delta^{1/10}\\right)\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\le C\\left(\\frac{L\\kappa}{\\alpha}\n+\\sqrt{L\\alpha}\n+\\ell L^{29}\\alpha^{-4}\\kappa^{-24}\\delta^{1/10}\\right).\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\), take\n\\(\\alpha=\\delta^{1/1040}\\), \\(\\kappa=\\alpha^2\\).\nThe three density exponents are \\(1/1040\\), \\(1/2080\\), and \\(1/20\\), proving the result. For remaining \\(\\delta\\), use \\(LM^2\\) and enlarge the absolute constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Unnumbered graph observation in the proof of original Lemma 9.1.", "statement": "<p>Unnumbered graph observation in the proof of original Lemma 9.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>A bipartite graph has parts of size at most \\(M\\), at least \\(\\eta M^2\\) edges, and a proper edge coloring with at most \\(M\\) colors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a subset of the first part of size at least \\(c\\eta M\\) such that any two of its vertices have at least \\(c\\eta^5M^3\\) four-step paths between them, allowing repetitions. For fixed endpoints, the ordered four-color tuples of these paths are distinct.</p>", "proof": "<p>Pad the second part to \\(\\lceil M\\rceil\\le2M\\) points and take a random neighborhood \\(Y\\). Then\n\\(\\mathbb E|Y|^2\\ge\\eta^2M^2/4\\).\nPairs with codegree less than \\(c_0\\eta^2M\\) have expected count inside \\(Y^2\\) at most \\(c_0\\eta^2M^2\\). Considering \\(|Y|^2-100\\) times that count gives a \\(Y\\) with \\(|Y|\\ge c\\eta M\\) and fewer than \\(|Y|^2/100\\) bad pairs.</p><p>Delete vertices with more than \\(|Y|/4\\) bad partners. Two retained vertices have at least \\(c|Y|\\) common good midpoints, each with at least \\(c_0\\eta^2M\\) choices of neighbor on each side, giving \\(c\\eta^5M^3\\) paths. Proper coloring reconstructs a path from its start and ordered colors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Unnumbered difference-cover construction in original Lemma 9.1.", "statement": "<p>Unnumbered difference-cover construction in original Lemma 9.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Restrict to \\(N_s\\le\\delta M^2\\), choose \\(\\delta\\le\\alpha/2\\), \\(0&lt;\\kappa&lt;1\\), and order scales by descending \\(R_s\\). Greedily form counting blocks of mass at most \\(\\alpha M^2\\); their number \\(J_0\\le3L/\\alpha\\).</p><p>On a common fine grid \\(h_0\\mathbb Z\\), for block maximum \\(R_b^+\\), put\n\\[\nN_0=\\lceil32B\\rceil,\\quad\n\\epsilon_b=h_0\\left\\lceil16\\nu R_b^+/h_0\\right\\rceil,\n\\]\n\\[\nU_b(t)=\\{6n\\cdot\\zeta+u:\n|n|_\\infty\\le tN_0,\\ u\\in h_0\\mathbb Z,\\ |u|\\le t\\epsilon_b\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Except for at most \\(\\kappa M^2\\) uncovered occurrences in each core role and block, incident labels are covered by at most\n\\[\nK_1=C(L/\\kappa)^6\n\\]\nsubsets \\(\\mathcal B\\), each having difference set covered by at most \\(K_1M\\) translates of \\(U_b(2)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>While uncovered mass in role \\(i\\) exceeds \\(\\kappa M^2\\), project to a simple graph of \\((i,j)\\)-pairs, retaining one scale witness per pair, and color by a third core role \\(k\\). Pair determination makes coloring proper, and there are at least \\((\\kappa/L)M^2\\) edges.</p><p>Lemma 12.1 gives \\(\\mathcal B_0\\) of size at least \\(c(\\kappa/L)M\\). Alternating the four annular relations along a four-step path cancels intermediate \\(i\\)- and \\(j\\)-labels. Division by \\(\\alpha_i\\in\\{\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm3\\}\\) gives\n\\[\na-a'\\in q(c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4)+U_b(1).\n\\]\nThe factor \\(36\\) ensures the divided lattice shifts remain multiples of six.</p><p>Pack \\(\\mathcal B_0-\\mathcal B_0\\) with differences outside \\(U_b(2)\\). Each packed difference has \\(c(\\kappa/L)^5M^3\\) distinct four-color witnesses; two packed differences cannot share one. There are at most \\(M^4\\) color tuples. Thus packing size is at most \\(C(L/\\kappa)^5M\\), and maximality gives a difference cover. Remove \\(\\mathcal B_0\\) and repeat at most \\(C(L/\\kappa)\\) times.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Unnumbered future-combination and slab argument in original Lemma 9.1.", "statement": "<p>Unnumbered future-combination and slab argument in original Lemma 9.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Discard occurrences missing an own-block covered label. A role is future if its label belongs to a cover subset in a later counting block; the side is always nonfuture.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Occurrences with fewer than three future roles have total mass at most\n\\(C\\sqrt{L\\alpha}M^2\\).\nThere are at most \\(4J_0^4K_1^3\\) combinations of a current block, three future core roles, and one later cover subset in each role. Each combination has mass \\(\\rho M^2\\) with\n\\[\n\\rho\\le C\\ell K_1L\\delta^{1/10}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Nonfuture label sets are disjoint over blocks in each core role. Let their sizes be \\(m_i^b\\), with \\(\\sum_bm_i^b\\le M\\); in a side role take \\(m_l^b=\\sum_{s\\in b}w_s\\). An assigned nonfuture pair contributes at most\n\\(\\min(\\alpha M^2,Lm_i^bm_j^b)\\). Sum using the geometric mean bound and Cauchy–Schwarz to obtain \\(\\sqrt{L\\alpha}M^2\\) per pair.</p><p>Fix a remaining combination and its future subsets \\(\\mathcal B_1,\\mathcal B_2,\\mathcal B_3\\). If \\(\\rho\\le C\\delta\\ell\\), the desired bound holds. Otherwise split ordered mass at quartiles, omitting at most three crossing scale bins. Each remaining range has mass at least \\(\\rho M^2/8\\). Put\n\\(\\Gamma=C_{\\rm gap}(d+1)^2\\nu/\\mu\\), and let \\(R^\\#\\) be the largest reciprocal scale of the second range.</p><p>The first range supplies anchor sets \\(\\mathcal B_{i0}\\) of size at least \\(c\\rho M/L\\), separated modulo reserved shifts by \\(\\Sigma R^\\#\\). The last gives a set \\(\\mathcal F\\) of distinct actual triples with \\(|\\mathcal F|\\ge c\\rho M^2/L\\). The third-range scale gap separates second-range scales from last and future scales by \\(\\Gamma\\). Thinning the second range leaves \\(q\\ge c\\rho/(\\delta\\ell)\\) \\(\\Gamma\\)-separated translator scales, with one triple \\(t_v\\) at each.</p><p>For a future neighborhood \\(U_i\\), subdividing coefficient and scalar intervals covers \\(U_i(t)\\) by \\((2t+1)^{d+1}\\) translates of \\(U_i(1)\\). Hence some odd \\(1\\le H_i\\le100(d+1)^2\\) satisfies\n\\(|U_i(H_i+2)|\\le2|U_i(H_i)|\\).\nThe anchor translates of \\(U_i(H_i)\\) are disjoint by reserved separation. With\n\\(Z_i=\\mathcal B_{i0}+U_i(H_i)\\), the difference cover gives\n\\[\n|Z_i-\\mathcal B_i|\\le2K_1M|U_i(H_i)|\n\\le C(K_1L/\\rho)|Z_i|.\n\\]</p><p>Choose nonempty \\(X_i\\subseteq Z_i\\) minimizing\n\\(r_i=|X_i-\\mathcal B_i|/|X_i|\\).\nThe paper’s minimal-growth argument gives\n\\[\n|X_i-m\\mathcal B_i|\\le r_i^m|X_i|.\n\\]\nIndeed, partition successive translates of \\(X_i\\) by their newly contributed points; minimality bounds new points in the corresponding translates of \\(X_i-\\mathcal B_i\\) by \\(r_i\\) times that number. Iterate.</p><p>One anchor translate contains at least \\(|X_i|/M\\) of \\(X_i\\). Packings of \\(m\\mathcal B_i\\) outside \\(U_i(2H_i)\\) therefore have size at most \\(r_i^mM\\), for \\(m=2,3\\).</p><p>Put \\(r_0=CK_1L/\\rho\\). Cover the first two coordinates of the slab in\n\\(2\\mathcal B_1\\times2\\mathcal B_2\\times2\\mathcal B_3\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_i\\alpha_ix_i+36n\\cdot\\zeta\\right|\\le2\\nu R^\\#,\n\\quad |n|_\\infty\\le2B,\n\\]\nby \\(r_0^2M\\) boxes each. Over a fixed pair, the third coordinate is constrained to one reserved-shift interval of width \\(C(d+1)^2\\nu R^\\#\\).\nIf \\(\\mathcal P\\) packs that local set outside \\(U_3(2H_3)\\), then\n\\(\\mathcal B_{30}+\\mathcal P\\subset3\\mathcal B_3\\) is still a packing, by anchor separation. Thus\n\\(|\\mathcal P|\\le Cr_0^3L/\\rho\\).\nThe whole slab is covered by at most\n\\(CK_1^7(L/\\rho)^8M^2\\) product boxes.</p><p>All \\(q|\\mathcal F|\\) outputs of \\(t_v+\\mathcal F\\) lie in this slab, and each box contains at most one. Within a translation, label separation at last-range scales excludes collisions. Between translations, the larger translator residual dominates the other translator and last-range residuals, giving a residual of magnitude between \\(9\\mu R_{\\rm big}/10\\) and \\(2\\nu R_{\\rm big}\\). A common box would convert it, up to scalar error at most \\(\\mu R_{\\rm big}/10\\), into a reserved pure shift, contradicting the forbidden gap.</p><p>Consequently\n\\[\nc\\frac{\\rho}{\\delta\\ell}\\frac{\\rho}{L}\n\\le CK_1^7(L/\\rho)^8,\n\\]\nso \\(\\rho^{10}\\le C\\delta\\ell K_1^7L^9\\), which implies the stated convenient bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 9.3, Powered estimate for core terms", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use a retained normalized attempt and one localization leaf from Propositions 10–11. There are either three or four core roles; any remaining role is a side. Apply the original cell minimum before subadditivity, and estimate numerical errors absolutely.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) and \\(C_4&lt;\\infty\\), with \\(1-\\varepsilon&gt;2/3\\), such that all at-least-three-core terms contribute at most\n\\[\nC_q\\eta^\\varepsilon P^{C_4}|R|+O(e^{-k}|R|).\n\\]\nThe constants are uniform in depth and attempt. The exponents \\(\\varepsilon,C_4\\) are fixed before choosing \\(q\\) close to two.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose\n\\[\np=1-\\varepsilon=(1-\\theta')+\\theta'p_0\n\\]\nwith sufficiently small absolute \\(\\theta'&gt;0\\). Interpolate Lemma 13.2:\n\\[\n\\sum_s(N_s^G)^p\n\\le(L_0M_i^GM_j^G)^{1-\\theta'}\n\\left[P^C(1+\\nu+\\mu^{-1}+\\Lambda)^C(M')^{2p_0}\\right]^{\\theta'}.\n\\]</p><p>Order amplitudes as \\(\\sigma_1\\ge\\sigma_2\\ge\\sigma_3\\ge\\sigma_4\\), and use roles \\(1,2\\) for the pair bound. At least one is core. Proposition 11 and side-square caps give\n\\[\n\\sum_GM_i^G\\le CP^C\\sigma_i^{-2},\n\\qquad \\sum_GM_i^G\\le D_*\\quad\\text{for core roles}.\n\\]\nExpand the maximum \\(M'\\) into four choices. Products of group counts have total exponent \\(2p&gt;1\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_G\\prod_i(m_i^G)^{\\beta_i}\n\\le\\prod_i\\left(\\sum_Gm_i^G\\right)^{\\beta_i}\n\\quad\\text{when }\\sum_i\\beta_i\\ge1.\n\\]\nThis follows by normalization, Hölder, and monotonicity of sequence norms.</p><p>Use the largest size-to-count cost \\(\\sigma_4^{-2}\\), and the core count cap in the first pair. After multiplication by \\((\\prod_i\\sigma_i)^p\\), amplitude dependence is bounded by \\(P^C\\) times\n\\[\n\\min(1,D_*\\sigma_1^2)^{1-\\theta'}\n\\sigma_1^{p-2(1-\\theta')}\n\\sigma_2^{p-2(1-\\theta')}\n\\sigma_3^p\\sigma_4^{p-4\\theta'p_0}.\n\\]\nThe four exponents sum to zero. Their first three partial sums are\n\\[\n-1+\\theta'(1+p_0),\\quad\n-2+2\\theta'(1+p_0),\\quad\n-1+\\theta'(1+3p_0),\n\\]\nall negative. Hence the three dyadic amplitude-gap sums converge. The common shift decays below \\(\\sigma_1=D_*^{-1/2}\\); above it, up to the retained \\(P^C\\) bound, there are only \\(O(1+\\log D_*+\\log P)\\) bins. Sum the finitely many orderings.</p><p>Frequency interpolation loses at most\n\\(\\mu^{-C\\theta'}\\nu^{C\\theta'}\\Lambda^{C\\theta'}\\) and logarithms, while the powered kernel gives\n\\(\\mu^p\\nu^{-Ap}\\Lambda^{-Ap}\\).\nTake \\(\\theta'\\) small so \\(p-C\\theta'&gt;0\\), then fixed derivative order \\(A\\) large. All dyadic frequency sums converge. Heights, types, patterns, and leaves cost \\(P^{C_4}\\).</p><p>For the principal cell bound \\(\\mathcal C_I\\),\n\\[\n\\min(\\eta,\\mathcal C_I)\\le\\eta^\\varepsilon\\mathcal C_I^p\n\\le\\eta^\\varepsilon\n\\sum_{\\rm configurations}(\\text{principal contribution})^p.\n\\]\nIntegrate the path estimate using\n\\[\n\\sum_I|I|F_I=\\int_R\\sum_{I\\ni x_*}F_I\\,dx_*.\n\\]\nAdd the separately estimated absolute errors from Lemma 13.1. Decrease \\(\\theta'\\) if necessary to retain \\(p&gt;2/3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Unnumbered reductions of Sections 9.1–9.2:", "statement": "<p>Unnumbered reductions of Sections 9.1–9.2: common successful heights, torus norms, and frequency tests.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix an actual cell \\(I=I_s\\), \\(x_*\\in I\\), group \\(G\\), and \\(m_0\\in\\{3,4\\}\\) core roles. Write \\(r=|I|\\), \\(R_s=r^{-1}\\). For a successful height \\(h\\), let \\(a_j\\) be the inherited representative of the selected predecessor, put\n\\[\n\\xi=\\vartheta/L_{h-\\Delta},\\qquad \\gamma_j^*=\\omega_{a_j}(x_*),\n\\qquad B_h'=D_*^5E_{h-\\Delta},\\qquad U_0=D_*^{250}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Apart from absolutely summable error \\(O(e^{-k}|R|)\\), the absolute cell form is bounded by sums of\n\\[\nC_A\\mu\\nu^{-A}\\Lambda^{-A}\n\\left(\\prod_{j=0}^3\\sigma_j\\right)N_s^G.\n\\]\nHere \\(0&lt;\\mu\\le1\\le\\nu\\), \\(\\Lambda\\) are dyadic; core \\(\\sigma_j\\) are component-size bins; \\(N_s^G\\) counts representative tuples passing the following tests, once per scale:</p><p class=\"li\">Four cores: with \\(Q=\\sum_j\\gamma_j^*\\), \\(T_0=\\sum_jj\\gamma_j^*\\), choose a fixed-rule reference\n  \\[\n  q'=Q+n\\cdot\\xi,\\quad t'=T_0+m\\cdot\\xi,\\quad\n  \\max(|q'|,|t'|)\\le U_0R_s,\n  \\]\n  within the full Fourier box. Set\n  \\[\n  \\mu\\asymp\\min\\left(1,\\min_j|t'-jq'|/R_s\\right),\\quad\n  \\nu\\asymp\\max(1,|q'|/R_s,|t'|/R_s),\n  \\]\n  discarding references with inner minimum below \\(U_0^{-1}\\). Put \\(\\Lambda=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Three cores, side slot \\(l\\): with\n  \\[\n  Q=\\sum_{j\\ne l}\\gamma_j^*,\\quad\n  T_l=\\sum_{j\\ne l}(j-l)\\gamma_j^*,\n  \\]\n  choose a reference \\(|T_l+m\\cdot\\xi|\\le U_0R_s\\), discard normalized magnitude below \\(U_0^{-1}\\), and set \\(\\mu,\\nu\\) from that magnitude. For a fresh side cutoff,\n  \\[\n  S'=\\zeta^{\\rho_l}e(-c_lp^{\\rho_l})S_{\\rm phys},\n  \\]\n  define\n  \\[\n  b_\\Lambda^2=\\mathbb E_{\\rho_l}\n  \\sum_{\\substack{k'\\in\\mathbb Z:\\\\\n  |k'+r(Q+n\\cdot\\xi)|\\le2\\Lambda\n  \\text{ for some }|n|_\\infty\\le B_h'}}\n  |\\widehat{S'}_I(k')|^2,\n  \\]\n  and take the side amplitude bin from \\(b_\\Lambda\\).</p><p>References and windows are independent of failure-history masks.</p>", "proof": "<p>Sample heights independently and uniformly in \\(\\mathcal H_{\\rm up}\\). A core tuple succeeds simultaneously with probability at least \\(1-2m_0\\delta_*\\). Keep its first success, obtaining an exact expectation identity. Assign every previous failure to its first failing role; there are at most \\(m_0^n\\) assignments after \\(n\\) failures. Coherence makes each role’s selected mask a union of full core subtotals in minimal CURRENT components, all in the successful predecessor.</p><p>A PRE path to the representative gives an integral shift \\(|n_j|_\\infty\\le D_*E_{h-\\Delta}\\), with slope error at most \\(D_*\\Xi/r_{v_b}\\). Pre-lag and bounded admission curvature make slope and quadratic Taylor errors tiny. Thus the selected physical argument is\n\\[\ne(c_jp^{\\rho_j}(y_j))\ne(\\gamma_j^*(y_j-x_*))g_j(\\xi(y_j-x_*))\n\\]\nup to \\(D_*^{-150}\\) times its coefficient sum. The true cutoff is periodic on this torus; then truncate it and the common progression multiplier within \\(B_h'\\).</p><p>A successful component contains a nonexceptional primary. Its pure-horizontal tiny rational space \\(K_B\\) gives the connected torus\n\\[\n\\mathbb H=(K_B\\cap\\mathbb Z^{\\dim\\theta_z})^\\perp.\n\\]\nFinite Fourier averages along \\(\\xi(I-x_*)\\) differ from Haar averages by tiny coefficient-sum errors: characters in \\(K_B\\) are nearly constant, all others integrate negligibly. Almost orthogonality and coefficient floors yield\n\\[\na_j':=\\|g_j\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb H)}\n\\le C\\left[\n\\sum_{\\mathcal D\\ {\\rm selected}}\n\\left(\\|A_{\\mathcal D}\\|_{2,I}\n+D_*^{-30}\\sum_{m\\ {\\rm core\\ in}\\ \\mathcal D}|\\alpha_m|\\right)^2\n\\right]^{1/2}.\n\\]\nDistinct progression-coordinate pairs on \\(\\mathbb H^2\\) are independent Haar variables, because their nonzero-integer-determinant maps are surjective on a connected torus. Interpolation gives the four-\\(L^2\\) Haar form estimate, also with bounded multipliers.</p><p>For four cores, expanding the torus multiplier samples the kernel Fourier transform at\n\\(r(Q+n\\cdot\\xi,T_0+m\\cdot\\xi)\\).\nTwo moderate pairs have the same character cosets modulo \\(K_B\\); replace their real frequencies by the fixed reference. The retained coset sum is a Haar coefficient bounded by \\(C\\prod_ja_j'\\). Other cosets have rapidly decaying kernel tails. The four marginals make the transform vanish on \\(t'=jq'\\), giving reference bound \\(C_A\\mu\\nu^{-A}\\).</p><p>For three cores, insert a fresh side cutoff divided by its constant expectation. Fourier integration in \\(t\\) gives \\(W_\\beta(h)\\) with derivatives bounded by \\(C_A\\mu\\nu^{-A}\\), using the anchor marginal at zero and integration by parts at large \\(\\beta\\). The retained vertical coefficient \\(D_m(u)\\) has \\(L^2(\\mathbb H)\\) norm at most \\(C\\prod_{j\\ne l}a_j'\\). Horizontal coset coefficients have this \\(\\ell^2\\) bound. Their reference real frequencies are separated by more than one. Pairing against side Fourier coefficients gives matrix decay\n\\[\nC_{A,A'}\\mu\\nu^{-A}\n(1+|k'+r(Q+n\\cdot\\xi)|)^{-A'}.\n\\]\nSchur’s inequality on radius-\\(\\Lambda\\) shells gives\n\\(C_A\\mu\\nu^{-A}\\Lambda^{-A}b_\\Lambda\\prod_{j\\ne l}a_j'\\).\nShells above \\(U_0\\), absent references, and near-vanishing-line tests are negligible.</p><p>All scalar errors are bounded by \\(CD_*^{-100}\\) times selected absolute coefficient products and, if present, local side size. Expand coefficient sums first: each pure tuple has total first-success weight one, so errors do not pay failure-mask count.</p><p>For an assignment with \\(n_j\\) failures in role \\(j\\),\n\\[\na_j'\\le C\\sigma_{\\mathcal F_j},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E(a_j')^2\\le C(2\\delta_*)^{n_j}\\sigma_{\\mathcal F_j}^2.\n\\]\nHölder with \\(m_0\\) factors gives geometric decay \\((2\\delta_*)^{n/m_0}\\). Choose \\(\\delta_*\\) small enough to sum \\(m_0^n\\) assignments and all \\(n\\). Group final heights, types, amplitudes, and tested representative tuples to obtain the statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Proposition 9.2, Counts for tested core tuples", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a retained path point, height, role types, frequency bands, and amplitude bins from Lemma 13.1. In each group put\n\\[\nM_i^G=|\\mathcal A_i^G|\\quad\\text{for a core role},\n\\]\nand, for a side role \\(l\\),\n\\[\nM_l^G=\\sigma_l^{-2}\\sum_{s:G\\ {\\rm used}}\n\\|S_{l,G,s}\\|_{2,I_s}^2.\n\\]\nLet \\(M'=\\max_iM_i^G\\), omitting groups with no nonzero counts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute \\(p_0\\in(0,1)\\) and\n\\(L_0\\le P^C(1+\\log(\\nu/\\mu))^C\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_sN_s^G\\le L_0\\min_{i\\ne j}M_i^GM_j^G,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_s(N_s^G)^{p_0}\n\\le P^C(1+\\nu+\\mu^{-1}+\\Lambda)^C(M')^{2p_0}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Identify representative \\(a\\) with \\(\\omega_a(x_*)\\); separation makes this injective in a fixed role and type. Fixing two core labels determines the rest: subtract the \\(Q,T_0\\) tests for four cores, or the \\(T_l\\) tests for three cores. The remaining linear systems have determinant magnitude at most three; bounded shifts and \\(O(\\nu\\max(R_s,R_t))\\) errors are excluded by hereditary separation unless alternative labels agree.</p><p>Every core triple obeys an annular test\n\\[\nc\\mu R_s\\le\\left|\\sum_{i\\ {\\rm in\\ triple}}(i-l')a_i+n\\cdot\\xi\\right|\n\\le C\\nu R_s,\\qquad |n|_\\infty\\le CB_h'.\n\\]\nFor four cores this is \\(t'-l'q'\\); for three it is the nonzero \\(T_l\\)-test. A fixed pair fixes the full tuple. Proposition 2 bounds its scale occurrences by \\(L_0\\), proving core-pair mass bounds.</p><p>For a fixed core label and scale in the side case, different incident triples have disjoint side windows. Overlap would combine their \\(Q\\) and \\(T_l\\) relations to determine the other two labels, again contradicting separation unless identical. Couple the fresh offset across windows; Parseval gives incidence at most\n\\(w_s=\\sigma_l^{-2}\\|S_{l,G,s}\\|_{2,I_s}^2\\).\nThis proves the core/side pair bound.</p><p>Apply Proposition 12 with \\(M=M'\\), \\(L=L_0\\), and \\(\\zeta=\\xi/36\\).\nSince\n\\[\n\\xi/36=6^{\\Delta-2}\\vartheta/L_h,\n\\]\nthe allowed shifts fit hereditary separation with\n\\(Q=\\lfloor E_h/6^{\\Delta-2}\\rfloor\\), \\(\\Sigma=\\Xi/2\\).\nThe choices of \\(A_0,B_h',\\Xi\\) give all reserves. A nonexceptional primary in the successful component supplies the pure-shift gap for every reserved shift, whether or not the representative is nonexceptional.</p><p>Thus, with \\(c'=1/2080\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{N_s^G\\le\\delta(M')^2}N_s^G\n\\le P^C(1+\\nu+\\mu^{-1}+\\Lambda)^C\n\\delta^{c'}(M')^2.\n\\]\nAlso \\(N_s^G\\le(M')^2\\). Choose \\(p_0=1-c'/2\\), split into density bands\n\\(2^{-j-1}(M')^2&lt;N_s^G\\le2^{-j}(M')^2\\), and sum the geometric ratio \\(2^{1-p_0-c'}&lt;1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 2.3, Normalized absorption estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For a suitable fixed \\(q\\in(2,3)\\), let \\(C_N=C_N(q)\\) be the finite constant defined in Theorem 1. A finite rooted subtree has root \\(I_*\\), all summation cells lie in at most \\(N\\) consecutive depths, and\n\\[\n\\|f_j\\|_{q,I}\\le1\n\\quad(0\\le j\\le3,\\ I\\text{ in the subtree}).\n\\]\nA rooted subtree contains every ancestor between a cell and its root; summation may use any subset.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently small fixed \\(c&gt;0\\), there is \\(A&lt;\\infty\\), independent of \\(N\\), such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{I\\ {\\rm summation\\ cell}}|I|\\,|H_I(f)|\n\\le(A+cC_N)|I_*|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a base segment, tuple, and active pattern from Proposition 5. Its later-level quantity is\n\\(\\sum_I|I|\\min(\\eta,|H_I(z)|)\\), \\(\\eta=e^{-k^\\theta}\\).</p><p>At an attempt root, construct main catalogs, colors, and ball filters with marking-failure cuts. Then construct fixed first-match modes and linear sides, choose offsets using the integrated physical-error bound, and close helpers on entire side states and named histories. Later size, top-count, power-budget, and hull-maximal stops act on these fixed objects. They remove original summands, not pieces of fixed stack functions.</p><p>Use first crossings of nonnegative partial ancestor quantities. Include all potential block ancestors before common cuts. Large-frozen-complement flags are powered errors; only their accumulated budget crossings cause restarts. Choose thresholds so the union of stopped roots has length at most \\(|R|/4\\). Restart original basic summands on those roots, retaining their inherited base data and reconstructing auxiliary sides. All root-local hypotheses hold anew. Lemma 1.1 gives total attempt-root length at most \\(4|R|/3\\).</p><p>Proposition 6 localizes. Proposition 10 supplies the linear-side square hypothesis; Lemma 11.1 separates cores, sides, and negligible physical errors. Proposition 13 handles at least three cores. Proposition 9 handles at least three sides, and, using Proposition 7, exactly two sides with whole complements. Restore the full pair in transferred main terms; errors have a third basic side. Keep coupled near-band sums at power one. Apply \\(\\min(\\eta,a)\\le\\eta^{1-p}a^p\\) to other terms, with \\(p=1-\\varepsilon\\) compatible with Propositions 9 and 13.</p><p>The retained estimate, including restarts, is\n\\[\nC_q\\left[\n\\eta^\\varepsilon P^{C_4}\n+(1+C_N)P^{-1/2}G_0(K+2)^{C_{19}(q-2)}\n\\right]|R|+O(e^{-k}|R|).\n\\]\nHere all displayed polynomial degrees are absolute before choosing \\(q\\), and\n\\[\n\\log D_{\\rm base},\\log D_m\\le C_qk^C,\\quad\n\\log D_*\\le C_q(1+k+P)^C,\n\\]\n\\[\nK\\le C_q(1+k+P)^{C_5},\\quad\nG_0\\le\\exp(C_q(1+\\log(2k))^C).\n\\]\nMain/helper counts do not depend on later mode precision. Mode graphs and cutoffs are budgeted before height and shift reserves, and \\(K\\) is chosen last. Strong side compression uses main catalogs only; the bad-density count removes the large height factor from side energy.</p><p>Choose \\(q&gt;2\\) close enough to two that\n\\[\nC_{19}C_5(q-2)&lt;1/8.\n\\]\nFor \\(P=\\lceil\\eta^{-\\alpha}\\rceil\\), fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), large \\(k\\) makes small overheads at most \\(P^{1/8}\\), so\n\\[\nP^{-1/2}G_0(K+2)^{C_{19}(q-2)}\n\\le C_qP^{-1/4}.\n\\]\nNext take \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) small enough that\n\\(\\alpha C_4&lt;\\varepsilon/2\\), together with the analogous powered-side bounds. Then\n\\[\n\\eta^\\varepsilon P^{C_4}\\lesssim\\eta^{\\varepsilon/2},\n\\qquad P^{-1/4}\\le\\eta^{\\alpha/4}.\n\\]\nIncrease the starting accuracy logarithm to meet all reserves. Since \\(\\theta&lt;1\\), numerical \\(e^{-k}\\) errors are smaller than the quality gain. Thus for some fixed \\(c_*&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{I\\ {\\rm active}}|I|\\min(\\eta,|H_I(z)|)\n\\le C_q\\eta^{c_*}(1+C_N)|R|.\n\\]</p><p>At later level \\(t\\), there are \\(O(t)\\) earlier-increment choices and boundedly many slot patterns. Common inheritance roots and further attempts pack uniformly. The total \\(1+C_N\\) coefficient is bounded by a fixed multiple of\n\\[\n\\sum_{t\\ge12}t\\exp(-c_*k_t^\\theta),\n\\qquad k_{t+1}=k_t^A.\n\\]\nThis converges and tends to zero as \\(k_1\\to\\infty\\). Absolute errors sum as well. Finitely many initial levels have depth-independent cost by Proposition 5.</p><p>Choose \\(k_1\\) so the \\(C_N\\) coefficient is at most the prescribed \\(c\\), and put all other constants in \\(A\\). First use a finite update cutoff; on the fixed finite tree its residual terms vanish by Lemma 5.2. Pass to the limit with the established bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, Outer reduction; its first conclusion is original Theorem 2.2, Uniform local estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the admissible local forms, \\(M_q\\), and \\(C_N(q)\\) of Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. The normalized absorption estimate of Proposition 14 implies that, for its suitable \\(q\\in(2,3)\\),\n   \\[\n   \\sup_NC_N(q)&lt;\\infty.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. This uniform local estimate implies the operator bound and bounded extension stated in Theorem 1.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 15.1 proves the uniform local estimate from Proposition 14. Lemma 15.2 recovers the continuous principal-value four-linear form from that estimate. Substituting \\(t\\mapsto-t\\) changes only the sign and gives the operator convention in Theorem 1.</p><p>Duality against compactly supported bounded \\(f_0\\) gives its \\(L^1\\) bound. Density of Schwartz functions in \\(L^3\\), trilinearity, and completeness of \\(L^1\\) give the unique bounded trilinear extension.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "First part of the proof of original Lemma 2.4.", "statement": "<p>First part of the proof of original Lemma 2.4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Assume Proposition 14. For arbitrary compactly supported \\(L^q\\) inputs, begin with disjoint roots at the largest interval size and set \\(a_{j,J}=\\|f_j\\|_{q,J}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A stopping decomposition gives\n\\[\nC_N\\le C A+C cC_N,\n\\]\nwith \\(C\\) uniformly bounded for \\(2&lt;q&lt;3\\). Choosing \\(c\\) sufficiently small therefore gives a bound independent of \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Zero root sizes give zero contribution. Otherwise stop at first strict descendants where an average of \\(|f_j|^q\\) exceeds \\(16a_{j,J}^q\\). Each slot’s stops have total length at most \\(|J|/16\\), so all stops have length at most \\(|J|/4\\).</p><p>On the intervening tree, divide \\(f_j\\) by \\(16^{1/q}a_{j,J}\\). Proposition 14 bounds its contribution by\n\\[\n16^{4/q}(A+cC_N)|J|\\prod_ja_{j,J}.\n\\]\nRepeat at stopped roots. The sparse sets \\(E_J\\), obtained by deleting next roots, are disjoint, have size at least \\(3|J|/4\\), and satisfy \\(M_qf_j\\ge a_{j,J}\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_J|J|\\prod_ja_{j,J}\n\\le\\frac43\\int\\prod_jM_qf_j.\n\\]\nTake the defining supremum for \\(C_N\\), choose \\(Cc&lt;1/2\\), and absorb.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Second part of the proof of original Lemma 2.4.", "statement": "<p>Second part of the proof of original Lemma 2.4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Assume \\(\\sup_NC_N(q)&lt;\\infty\\) for \\(2&lt;q&lt;3\\). Choose an odd compactly supported Gevrey function \\(\\varphi\\) with \\(\\varphi^{(3)}(0)\\ne0\\), and compactly supported Gevrey \\(\\omega\\) with\n\\(\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb Z}\\omega(X-m)=1\\). Define\n\\[\nW(X,S)=\\prod_{j=0}^3(\\partial_S-j\\partial_X)[\\omega(X)\\varphi(S)].\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The local kernels built from \\(W\\) recover a nonzero constant multiple of \\(1/t\\), preserve all four vanishing marginals, and give\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\operatorname{p.v.}\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\nf_0(x)\\prod_{j=1}^3f_j(x+jt)\\,\\frac{dx\\,dt}{t}\n\\right|\n\\le C_q\\|f_0\\|_\\infty\\prod_{j=1}^3\\|f_j\\|_3\n\\]\nfor compactly supported bounded \\(f_0\\) and Schwartz \\(f_1,f_2,f_3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Each differential factor is differentiation along a marginal integration line, so compact support gives all four zero marginals. Spatial summation and substitution give\n\\[\n\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb Z}W(x/a-m,t/a)=\\varphi^{(4)}(t/a),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty\\varphi^{(4)}(t/a)\\,\\frac{da}{a^2}\n=-\\frac{\\varphi^{(3)}(0)}t,\\qquad t\\ne0.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(a=b2^n\\), \\(1\\le b&lt;2\\). For fixed \\(b\\), put each\n\\(a^{-1}W(x/a-m,t/a)\\) in the form \\(r^{-1}w_I\\), \\(r=Da\\), for fixed sufficiently large dyadic \\(D\\). A fixed finite family of shifted dyadic lattices, with scale boundaries at offsets \\(0,r/3,-r/3\\) and alternating signs for nesting, places all four progression coordinates in one cell with the required interior margin. Each cell receives boundedly many translates. Fix admissibility constants to cover the rescaled kernels.</p><p>The uniform local estimate bounds finite scale and spatial sums. Integrate \\(db/b\\). Hölder and \\(L^3\\)-boundedness of \\(M_q\\), since \\(q&lt;3\\), give\n\\[\nC\\int\\prod_{j=0}^3M_qf_j\n\\le C_q\\|f_0\\|_\\infty\\prod_{j=1}^3\\|f_j\\|_3.\n\\]\nCompact truncations justify the defining input class: finite scales and compact support in slot zero force all relevant coordinates into a fixed compact set.</p><p>After spatial summation the kernel is odd. Pair positive and negative \\(t\\) near zero; the Schwartz-product difference is \\(O(|t|)\\), uniformly on the support of \\(f_0\\). Large-\\(|t|\\) Schwartz decay is integrable. Integrated scale kernels are bounded by \\(C/|t|\\), so dominated convergence recovers the principal-value form.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 6, "cite"], [1, 18, "cite"], [1, 19, "cite"], [1, 26, "cite"], [1, 32, "cite"], [1, 37, "cite"], [1, 49, "cite"], [2, 22, "cite"], [2, 61, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 18, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 19, "cite"], [5, 25, "cite"], [5, 43, "cite"], [5, 60, "cite"], [6, 22, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 27, "cite"], [7, 51, "cite"], [8, 12, "cite"], [8, 15, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [9, 19, "cite"], [9, 25, "cite"], [9, 26, "cite"], [9, 27, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [10, 19, "cite"], [10, 20, "cite"], [10, 25, "cite"], [10, 42, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [11, 20, "cite"], [12, 6, "cite"], [12, 19, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 6, "cite"], [17, 6, "cite"], [18, 24, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 18, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [21, 26, "cite"], [21, 33, "cite"], [21, 51, "cite"], [21, 61, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 61, "cite"], [24, 21, "cite"], [25, 21, "cite"], [26, 61, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [27, 30, "cite"], [27, 32, "cite"], [27, 33, "cite"], [27, 42, "cite"], [28, 26, "part"], [29, 26, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [30, 37, "cite"], [30, 41, "cite"], [30, 49, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 26, "cite"], [33, 36, "cite"], [33, 39, "cite"], [33, 51, "cite"], [33, 61, "cite"], [34, 36, "cite"], [35, 34, "cite"], [36, 41, "cite"], [36, 51, "cite"], [36, 61, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [37, 39, "cite"], [37, 55, "cite"], [37, 56, "cite"], [37, 57, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"], [39, 36, "part"], [40, 36, "cite"], [41, 50, "cite"], [41, 51, "cite"], [41, 58, "cite"], [41, 61, "cite"], [42, 41, "cite"], [43, 41, "cite"], [43, 44, "cite"], [44, 41, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [44, 52, "cite"], [45, 41, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 41, "cite"], [48, 41, "cite"], [48, 50, "cite"], [48, 53, "cite"], [49, 41, "cite"], [49, 50, "cite"], [50, 58, "cite"], [51, 50, "cite"], [51, 61, "cite"], [52, 50, "cite"], [53, 50, "cite"], [54, 60, "cite"], [55, 56, "cite"], [56, 54, "cite"], [57, 54, "cite"], [58, 61, "cite"], [59, 58, "cite"], [59, 60, "cite"], [60, 58, "cite"], [61, 0, "cite"], [61, 62, "cite"], [61, 63, "cite"], [62, 0, "cite"], [63, 62, "cite"], [64, 62, "cite"]]});
